{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat review-inputs/1792143479000-orchestrator-6d-plan-v2-mob-consensus.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-16 09:38:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-16 16:38:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: zcode (interactive seat)\nSession-ID: sess_607c1129-1dcd-49ba-9b34-58565f4f42e4\n# Task: mob consensus on Fable's 6d plan v2 — orchestrator reconciliation (three reviewers: codex gpt-6-astra, muse muse-wonder-1.0, glm glm-5.3)\nRole: Lead Engineering Orchestrator\nImplementers:\n- Model: GLM-5.3-Flash (orchestrator) | Assigned: 2026-10-16 16:06 +07 | Status: completed | Rationale: owner ordered the mob; reviewers' findings in collab/1792141480000-architect-6d-plan-v2-mob-<codex|muse|glm>.*.findings.md; this file is the reconciled consensus, with the orchestrator's own source checks against origin/master@c6bc91f7\n\n## Verdicts and the reconciled outcome\n\nRaw verdicts: codex-astra **NOT-READY** (4 P1, 6 P2; defaults 6 and 8 UNSAFE as stated), muse **NOT-READY** (2 P1, 1 P2 refuted-candidate, 2 P3; all defaults SAFE), glm-5.3 **IMPLEMENTATION-READY** (1 P2, 4 P3; all defaults SAFE).\n\n**Reconciled: NOT-READY for 6d-a as v2 specifies — but close.** All three validate the architecture itself (task product, slots 17/18, kind rules, fuel concept, the four tenure outcomes, jump/terminator layouts, harness order); none found a design-level flaw. The blockers are specification-level and small. glm's READY does not survive the two P1s the other reviewers found INDEPENDENTLY and the orchestrator verified in source (glm's otherwise broad citation sweep — 60+ sites — did not examine the bridge loop's budget, the vm-hot entry guard, or the completed-install pending rule). Two-of-three NOT-READY with the third's misses identified: the mob's judgment is NOT-READY with the amendment list below.\n\n## P1 amendments required before 6d-a (all verified in source by the orchestrator)\n\n1. **Bound `run-fuel` through the `ffi/maybe-run` bridge loop** (codex #3 = muse N1; consensus). `maybe-run` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:338-350`) recurs `(run-fn vm)` after every handled bridge step with a fresh budget — a bridge-served machine never returns from one `run-fuel` call, so §3.4's \"at most n instructions per task per program tick\" is false as stated. Fix: carry remaining fuel across bridge iterations or return after one bridge cycle (a `maybe-run` variant), plus an FFI-loop acceptance case in 6d-a item 9.\n2. **Move the `vm-hot` entry gate guard from 6d-b to 6d-a** (codex #4 = muse N3; consensus). `vm-hot` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:135-148`) has no gate check; 6d-a's fenced `:running` restore (control set, gate `:exporting`) is steppable until 6d-b ships the guard, destroying rehydrate byte-equality. Fix: one `contains?` read at entry (also on `vm/step`/`vm/run` paths per codex), guard moved into 6d-a's change list, \"fenced running machine refuses vm/step\" pinned in 6d-a acceptance.\n3. **Define the completed-outcome grammar for link and install wakes** (codex #1; muse and glm ruled F1 ANSWERED and missed it — orchestrator verification sustains codex). The kernel queues install completions (`wake-installed`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1327-1345`) and link wakes (`poll-links`, `:1627-1645`) into `:ready-queue` TODAY, and completion REMOVES the install (`:1350`, `:1454` `(update :installs dissoc module-name)`). v2's `:ready` row admits only `:next`/`:put` reasons (a queued `:link-response` wake would refuse `:ready-reason`), and its \"ready wait validated in context `:wait` exactly as a pending wait\" routes an install completion through `pending-node`'s `:install` arm (`rows.cljc:1563-1565`) which refuses `:incomplete-install` when the install is absent. Fix: completed-outcome validation decoupled from outstanding-pending validation (codex's minimal fix), ready reasons widened to every existing queue producer (link success/refusal incl. `:try?` outcomes, install success/refusal), queue-plus-active tests after install completion and link refusal. Belongs in 6d-a.\n\n## P2 amendments (adopt into v2.1)\n\n4. **Ready-row cursor-cell encoding** (codex #2 vs muse N2 vs glm N1 — all three converge from different angles). Adjudication: muse's mechanism analysis is right (check-wait-set advances shared cursors to the final successor before any resume, so intermediate bodies are unobservable and first-wins `cell!` aliasing is consistent); glm's concrete fix is the one to adopt — the successor body is emitted as its OWN `:cursor-cell-body` row paired under the same canonical C (not a second `cell!` registration), with the decode rule that one C may pair with two bodies across the task row and its ready rows, restore mapping the ready row's C through the slot-9 cell map; v2 §3.2's \"two positions of one cell id\" sentence is struck as wrong; muse's shared-cursor pinning case (two waiters, snapshot after sweep before resume, law 1 + 2) added to acceptance item 3.\n5. **Completed `:put` op-id: separate shape validation from retained-write custody validation** (codex #5 + muse N5; glm's default-6 analysis shows `with-custody` reads slot 13 only, so ready rows are outside numbering/`protect!` by construction). Name the site (e.g. `check-pendings!` over slot 18 reusing `check-op-id!` for form only) and pin one malformed-op-id refusal.\n6. **Drop the acceptance-7 cardinality equality** `|layout(p)| + |uses(p)| = |L(p)|` \"with duplicates counted\" (codex #6; `analysis.cljc` defines L as a set — duplicates cannot raise its cardinality; tail ambient operands break it unconditionally). Replace with the ordered region length against the ordered operand/layout sequence, repeated-register agreement, and the set equations separately.\n7. **Ready `:V` enters the full-payload refusal oracle** (codex #7): a primitive nested in a queued outcome becomes live only at resume; include it in both the implementation traversal and the independent oracle, with the register-target case where the only unsupported value is in the queued outcome.\n8. **Exception-state channel for tenure cuts** (codex #8): v2's \"assoc before the next tenure check\" does not update `step-active`'s catch closure (`driver.cljc` catch closes over its original argument). Return explicit stage outcomes carrying the latest state, or move catches to stage boundaries; pin exact retained-machine equality at each tenure cut.\n9. **UCF export step 1 \"Quiesce: the ready-queue must be empty\" (`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1904`) is missing from the §3.7.3/§3.8 amendment inventory** (codex #10; orchestrator-verified it exists and is unlisted). Replace with retention of the ordered queue in the export record.\n\n## P3 amendments (fold into the brief; do not re-open design)\n\nmuse O1 / glm N3: re-base all file:line references on post-module-pins master or locate by symbol + quoted text (offsets are unusable: measured drift task-ops +7 … restore-task kind arms +59; two tuples.md citations land in the wrong text). muse N4/glm O3: the blocked+ready coverage class is unreachable by a `run-fuel 1` driver — strike it from the driver's class list (pin via items 3d/6) or specify a round-phase seam. muse N5/glm item above: the op-id site (P2 #5). glm N2: §3.2's slot-18 `lower-defect` expression is garbled (double dereference) — resolve the ready row's wait cref to its wait row first. glm N4: C3's diagnostic is `:pc-body`, not `:body-continuity`. glm N5: one sentence + test for halted-with-outstanding-wait and empty queue (classifies `:blocked`, halt value dropped as today). glm omissions: a terminal-status ready row law-1 case (`terminal-resume-outcome` raises identically on the receiver); one sentence on `run-fuel`'s inactive predicate vs `vm-hot`'s (a quiescent ingress-ready machine takes the unbudgeted `scheduler-round`). codex #9: holds' \"resolve within one cycle\" is not guaranteed (reader backpressure, `reader.cljc:484-496` — orchestrator did not re-verify) — reword default 8 accordingly rather than claim the guarantee.\n\n## The eight §3.10 defaults — consensus rulings\n\n1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7: **SAFE as stated** (3×SAFE across reviewers; no dissent).\n6 (completed `:put` wakes): **SAFE in policy, UNSAFE as mechanically specified** (codex) — ratify WITH the P2 #5 amendment (shape-vs-custody separation, named site). muse and glm rule the policy itself sound; no reviewer defends v2's literal mechanism.\n8 (holds stay refusals): **ratify WITH rewording** — codex shows backpressure can hold an observation across cycles indefinitely; the default's own \"verify each resolves within one cycle, else known-gaps row\" duty stands, but the documents must not promise the one-cycle resolution.\n\n## Recommendation to the owner\n\nRatify defaults 1-5, 7 as stated and 6/8 with the amendments above; commission plan v2.1 (the amendment list in this file is complete and specified enough to apply mechanically); then dispatch 6d-a from the drafted brief updated with the P1/P2 items and the brief-hygiene P3s. Fable authored v2 and is out until Tue 2026-10-20: per the standing rule, the row-grammar/custody MERGE should wait for Fable's sign-off (or an owner ruling on which reviewer counts) — the implementation itself can start earlier on a non-codex seat (codex is quota-limited until ≈17:45 +07 per routing-status).\n\n## Process\n\nReviewers ran read-only against /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-review-6dv2 (origin/master@c6bc91f7, kept until v2.1/6d-a routing is decided). Sessions: codex 01a143f7-0693-7a32-9229-1fccbcc526a9; muse 95894b11-b8e6-4d6c-9bdd-049f3803fcba (one resume past its plan-approval gate); glm e33f308d-2ca8-4019-917d-4c85854673b4. The orchestrator independently verified: ffi.cljc:338-350, semantic.cljc:135-148, engine.cljc:1327-1345/:1627-1645/:1350/:1454, rows.cljc:1387-1401/:1540-1565/:397/:468, UCF :1904.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
