{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat review-inputs/1792141480000-architect-6d-plan-v2-mob-muse.muse-wonder-1-0.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-16 09:14:43 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-16 16:14:43 +07\nCoding-Agent: muse\nSession-ID: 95894b11-b8e6-4d6c-9bdd-049f3803fcba\n# Task: mob consensus review of the 6d plan v2 (any-pc snapshots) — muse muse-wonder-1.0 findings\nRole: Architect\nImplementers:\n- Model: muse-wonder-1.0 | Assigned: 2026-10-16 16:06 +07 | Status: completed | Rationale: one of three mob reviewers (codex gpt-6-astra, muse muse-wonder-1.0, glm glm-5.3); reviewed Fable's 6d plan v2 against origin/master@c6bc91f7 read-only; first turn stopped at the plan-approval gate (headless), resumed once (--resume, same session) with instructions to deliver; final message promoted verbatim below by the orchestrator\n\nMOB-VERDICT: NOT-READY\n\nMob review (muse seat) of slice 6d plan v2, verified read-only against `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-review-6dv2` (origin/master @ c6bc91f7, incl. module-pins commit 2). The owner invariant, the requirement for 6d, and Fable's authorship were taken as given and not re-litigated. Verdict is for 6d-a under the §3.10 defaults as literally specified; the two P1s below are small and local, and after them I would expect IMPLEMENTATION-READY.\n\n## Citation sample (18 checked; all held in substance, with post-module-pins line drift)\n\nv2's citations were read at `7c4dff32`; this tree is after module-pins commit 2, so rows.cljc has drifted ≈ +28 lines and driver.cljc ≈ +6. I report current-tree lines; in every case the cited code does what v2 claims.\n\n1. `engine.cljc:2205-2219` run-loop steps the active activation before the queue — HELD.\n2. `engine.cljc:2294-2312` resume-from-run-queue pops one entry, clears blocked/halted, merges `:resource-updates`, does not touch `:wait-set` — HELD.\n3. `engine.cljc:442-463` woken entries carry `:value :status :resource-updates :cursor` — HELD.\n4. `engine.cljc:1475,1490-1491` advance-install runs the child with `vm/run`; a `:running` phase arm already exists — HELD.\n5. `engine.cljc:480` stream construction calls the composition constructor synchronously — HELD.\n6. `engine.cljc:2135-2199` check-wait-set: link polling, successor-cursor writeback for shared cursors, ready-queue append — HELD.\n7. `semantic.cljc:50-55` put-registers materializes control/window/env/k; `:116-118` register-restore sets control without clearing waits — HELD.\n8. `semantic.cljc:134-143` vm-hot materializes the boundary at fuel exhaustion; `:193` host call is one `(apply f args)` — HELD.\n9. `semantic/layout.cljc:118-131` stack-layouts assigns only parse-node pcs; `:853-874` frame-prim/state-prim traverse window + lexical bindings + K; `:947-951` lower-defect feeds `(first (:slots w))` from slot 13 — HELD.\n10. `semantic/code.cljc:389-396` a conditional's `:jump` is metadata, not a child; `:413-414` the terminator is outside the expression — HELD.\n11. `semantic/analysis.cljc:45-67,86-97` backward liveness at every pc, `saved = L − {rd}`; `semantic/derive.cljc:127-143` allocation over `L ∪ def`, slots freed only when interval end < pc — HELD.\n12. `debruijn/stack.cljc:689-690` const pushes; `:722-723` `:push` only advances pc; `:731,747` a tail call keeps `stack'` — HELD.\n13. `ucf/rows.cljc:1680-1685` (tree lines) ready-queue `:not-quiescent` refusal and control `:not-at-safepoint` refusal both present as v2 describes; `:1890-1891` install child kind must be `:blocked`; `:2071-2087` check-custody skips the grant only when fenced or halted (F6 site, correct as written); `:1732-1744` header-custody four-key arm for any non-halted kind — HELD.\n14. `ucf/holder/driver.cljc` run-cycle: require-tenure → m1 → require-tenure before the assoc, and step-active's catch ends the run on the pre-slice state (tree `:2117-2150`, `:2246-2262`) — HELD (F4 root cause).\n15. `ucf/holder/export.cljc:107-114` enter's first-refusal on a non-empty ready queue — HELD.\n16. `ffi.cljc:334-350` maybe-run loops bridge steps while blocked, with no budget or iteration parameter — HELD (basis of N1 below).\n17. Design docs: `yin.vm.semantic.md:680-689` explicit park is a no-wait safepoint and the quiescence sentence stands; `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:367-369,1248-1252` three-kind custody prose and refusal rows incl. the stepped-kernel refusal; `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:473-478` kind rules; `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:799-821` safepoint-only totality; `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:602,622` static-safepoint-set checks — all HELD.\n18. `docs/agents/known-gaps.md` (full file read): no any-pc row yet — HELD; v2's proposed row is still to be added.\n\n## (2) The nine findings\n\n- F1: ANSWERED (v2 §3.1 `:ready` row and task product; §3.2 lift/lower; acceptance §3.9 6d-a item 3c)\n- F2: ANSWERED (v2 §3.1 kind table: active control with ordered waits and ordered ready outcomes; R0 withdrawn)\n- F3: NOT-ANSWERED (children are budgeted in §3.4, but one execution path inside a single `run-fuel` call remains unbounded: the `ffi/maybe-run` bridge loop — see N1; the §3.4 per-tick bound sentence is therefore false as stated for bridge-served machines)\n- F4: ANSWERED (v2 §3.3 outcomes 1–3: assoc each stage before the next tenure check, retained boundary inspection-only, expiry recovers the last durable checkpoint and the loss is stated in the documents)\n- F5: ANSWERED (v2 §3.5: jump layout `layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]`, `[]` at terminators, ordered region check, dead-prefix rule for tail residuals; §3.9 item 7 static gate)\n- F6: ANSWERED (v2 §3.1: `check-custody!` dispatch preserved unchanged; halted-without-grant pinned as a 6d-b regression)\n- F7: ANSWERED (v2 §3.6: refusal oracle is an independent traversal over the whole payload — run window, lexical bindings, every K frame, waits, ready states, parked states; §3.2 lower-defect over slots 17/18)\n- F8: ANSWERED (v2 §3.6: lift → lower → immediate relift/law 2 → run/law 1, isolated worlds from a deterministic script; the residual coverage-class problem is N4, a new defect, not F8's order/worlds point)\n- F9: ANSWERED (v2 §3.7 exact amended text separating representable/portable/satisfied/export-ready, corrected safepoint definition, `:push` corrected to a stutter; §3.8 line-granular inventory — but see omission O1: the inventory's lines are already stale on this tree)\n\n## (3) New defects in v2\n\n**N1 — P1 — `run-fuel`'s bounded-return contract fails through `ffi/maybe-run`.** Plan §3.4 defines `run-fuel` as `(ffi/maybe-run machine (fn [m] …))` and guarantees (§3.4, honest-guarantee paragraph) interruption \"after at most `n` instructions of each task of its tree per program tick.\" But `maybe-run` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:334-350`) loops — `(recur (run-fn vm))` after every answered bridge step — with no budget, iteration cap, or return to the caller. A bridge-served program that keeps making FFI requests (each iteration individually fuel-bounded, the loop not) never returns from one `run-fuel` call, so the holder never regains control: exactly the unbounded-in-one-tick class F3 required closing, one seam over. The plan's bullet \"each loop costs another slice\" concedes the iterations exist but never bounds them per call. Minimal fix: give `run-fuel` a bounded bridge loop — e.g. return the machine after one bridge cycle and let the holder re-invoke, or thread a call-level slice budget into a `maybe-run` variant — and add an acceptance case (an FFI-looping program preempted by `run-fuel`) to 6d-a item 9.\n\n**N2 — REFUTED as a code defect; the plan's mechanism is right and one of its sentences is wrong (P3 documentation).** My candidate was that the first-wins census (`ucf/rows.cljc:1388-1393`, `cell!` registers only the first body per cell id) would drop a `:ready` row's successor cell. Reading the lift order refutes it: a ready entry's pending registers its cell via `cell-for!` (`:1542-1544`), which reads the machine's own `:resources` — and `check-wait-set` (`engine.cljc:2192-2199`) has already advanced those resources to the final successor before any entry is queued, so every registration of one cell id in one lift reads the identical current body and first-wins aliasing is consistent; carried values, not re-reads, deliver the intermediate elements, in queue order. What is wrong is v2 §3.2's sentence claiming \"the task's `:cursor-cells` row holds the current, the ready row the successor; two positions of one cell id\" — there are not two positions; there is one canonical (final) body, and that is precisely why it works. Minimal fix: amend that sentence in §3.2 to say the ready row references the census's single current cell body, and pin the semantics with a shared-cursor case (two waiters on one cursor, snapshot after the sweep before any resume) in 6d-a acceptance item 3. No code change needed.\n\n**N3 — P1 — The kernel gate guard is sequenced one slice too late.** Plan §3.2 gives 6d-a the restore: a `:running` task restores with `:control` set, and \"a fenced restore puts the ready list in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`… and leaves `:ready-queue []`\" — but nothing strips or parks the control. The existing fenced arm (`ucf/rows.cljc:2109-2111`) moves only waits and parked records into recovery. The `vm-hot` entry guard that makes \"the exporting machine is pure data\" true is assigned to 6d-b (§3.3 outcome 4; §3.9 6d-b item 3). So throughout 6d-a, `lower-task {:fenced true}` on a running root yields a machine with control set and gate `:exporting` that `vm-hot` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:137-143`, no gate check) will happily step — pure computation advances, windows mutate, and rehydrate byte-equality is destroyed. The second review explicitly required kernel fencing to ship with the first exposed running path. Minimal fix: move the one-check `vm-hot` entry guard from 6d-b item 3 into 6d-a (it is a single `contains?` read at entry, outside the instruction loop), and add the \"fenced running machine refuses `vm/step`\" assertion to 6d-a acceptance instead of only 6d-b.\n\n**N4 — P2 — One §3.6 coverage class is unreachable by the driver §3.6 defines.** §3.6 requires the stepping driver to pin coverage by structural class including \"scheduler product (… blocked + ready)\" and asserts \"each class is reached at least once on the corpus\"; §3.9 item 4 defines that driver as `run-fuel 1` boundaries only. But `run-fuel` on a blocked machine performs the entire budgeted round — sweep, budgeted installs, and the resume (§3.4) — so the post-sweep/pre-resume blocked+ready state never materializes as a returned boundary. Item 3(d) in fact constructs that state by calling `check-wait-set` directly, outside the driver. As written, the coverage assertion in §3.6/§5.7 cannot pass. Minimal fix: either pin blocked+ready solely through the products test (items 3d/6) and strike it from the driver's class list, or give the harness an explicit round-phase seam and say so in §3.6. (The companion re-examination — halted-beside-queue reclassified `:blocked` in §3.1 — I checked and it is CORRECT, not a defect: `run-loop` (`engine.cljc:2216-2219`) reads the queue before the halt, a terminal ready entry throws in `resume-from-run-queue` (`:2310-2311`) before any halt value is read, and any resumed activation overwrites the halt when it next parks or halts; the halt value is dead by the kernel's own order.)\n\n**N5 — P3 — The completed-`:put` op-id well-formedness check has no site.** §3.1's `task-ops` bullet says a ready `:put`'s op-id \"is checked well-formed (`check-op-id!`) but excluded from `:yin.k/ops`\". In the current code `check-op-id!` is called from inside `task-ops` itself (`ucf/rows.cljc:1286-1291`); excluding ready rows from `task-ops` removes the only caller, and no other stage in §3.1's validator table is named as performing the check (acceptance item 2 tests row shape and status-keyword, not op-id form). A malformed op-id inside a carried ready `:put` pending would therefore pass lower silently — harmless to the receiver (it delivers `:V`, never re-appends), but the plan's stated check does not exist as specified. Minimal fix: name the site — e.g. `check-pendings!` over slot 18 reuses `check-op-id!` for form only — and add one malformed-op-id ready-row refusal to acceptance item 2.\n\n## (4) The eight §3.10 defaults\n\n(As I treat them: 1. task slots 17 `:run` / 18 `:ready` with a one-time re-mint after module-pins; 2. fuel as holder config `:fuel`, default nil; 3. no automatic export at the bound in 6d-b, explicit `hand-off` only; 4. no adoption of a retained boundary, recovery is the last durable checkpoint; 5. keep edge-reachability liveness (Q6) and pin the pre-branch `ghost` false refusal; 6. completed `:put` wakes are completed operations, excluded from retained writes and `protect!`; 7. `:push` relabelled to its successor, not refused; 8. holds stay refusals as documented driver transients.)\n\n1. **SAFE** — separate slots remove F7's silent-misread class by construction (every slot-13 traversal stays wait-only), and the forced re-mint coincides with module-pins' re-mint that has already landed on this tree.\n2. **SAFE** — fuel as an opt-in composition knob keeps clocks and time-conversion out of the VM while making preemption available; the invariant closes only for compositions that set it, and the plan says so rather than hiding it (subject to N1's bound actually holding).\n3. **SAFE** — `hand-off` is already the composition's explicit act in the driver, `:yin.k/at-boundary` supplies the signal, and a later tenure-aware auto-arm policy needs no carrier or VM change.\n4. **SAFE** — an ended holder holds no tenure, so adoption would be new ledger authority; retaining the boundary for inspection only, with the checkpoint-loss sentence written into the documents, is the honest specified outcome F4 demanded.\n5. **SAFE** — the `ghost` refusal is a bounded, documented discharge conservatism (`:yin.k/unsatisfied` at a pc whose segment cannot be discharged), not a scheduler-state incompleteness; removing it is a separate constant-propagation slice and should not gate 6d-a.\n6. **SAFE** — the `:put` already appended under the source's own authority before the snapshot; counting it as a retained write would make the receiver re-append a datom already on the stream, duplicating the effect.\n7. **SAFE** — `debruijn/stack.cljc:722-723` shows `:push` only advances pc with the value already on the stack, so relabelling to the successor runs no guest semantics and loses nothing.\n8. **SAFE** — holds are driver-internal observations mid-step, not task states at rest, and the default as stated carries its own verification duty (6d-b proves each resolves within one cycle without guest execution, or files a known-gaps row).\n\n## (5) What 6d-a needs that the plan omits\n\n- **O1 — Re-base §3.8 before writing the brief.** The line-granular inventory and every `file:line` in §3.7/§3.9 were read at `7c4dff32`; on this tree rows.cljc is already ≈ +28 lines (e.g. the ready-queue refusal is at `:1680`, `check-custody!` at `:2071`, the kind rules in tuples moved behind the module-pins ruling insert). 6d-a's first task must be re-running the `grep safepoint` inventory and re-pinning all references on post-module-pins master, or implementers will edit by stale line numbers.\n- **O2 — The two P1 fixes folded into 6d-a's scope:** the bounded bridge loop for `run-fuel` (N1, with its FFI-loop acceptance case) and the `vm-hot` gate guard moved from 6d-b (N3, with the fenced-running-step refusal pinned in 6d-a). Without O2, 6d-a ships a preemption API whose bound is false for bridge-served machines and a fenced running machine that can be stepped.\n- **O3 — A decision on the blocked+ready harness seam** (N4): strike the class from the §3.6 driver coverage list (pinning it via items 3d/6 only) or specify the round-phase seam the harness will use; the acceptance text must not assert a class its own driver cannot reach.\n- **O4 — The shared-cursor ready case and the §3.2 sentence correction** (N2): two waiters on one cursor, snapshot after the sweep before any resume, law 1 + law 2 — this is the one ready-carrier aliasing case the F1/F2 acceptance cases (distinct callers/streams) do not exercise.\n- **O5 — A named op-id form check for ready `:put` pendings** (N5) with one malformed-row refusal test, so the §3.1 claim is implemented where the brief says it is.\n\nSummary of the seat's position: v2 answers eight of the nine findings completely and F3 in all but one seam; the grammar, product state model, validator, custody, expiry, stack-layout, and harness-order work is sound and well-evidenced (N2 and the halted-beside-queue rule were checked and are correct). It is NOT-READY as literally specified because N1 leaves `run-fuel`'s central bound false and N3 leaves a steppable exporting machine exposed for the whole of 6d-a — both are one-paragraph amendments to §3.4/§3.9 plus one moved guard, after which this seat would return IMPLEMENTATION-READY without further design review.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
