{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat review-inputs/1792141480000-architect-6d-plan-v2-mob-glm.glm-5-3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-16 09:35:32 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-16 16:35:32 +07\nCoding-Agent: glm\nSession-ID: e33f308d-2ca8-4019-917d-4c85854673b4\n# Task: mob consensus review of the 6d plan v2 (any-pc snapshots) — glm glm-5.3 findings\nRole: Architect\nImplementers:\n- Model: glm-5.3 | Assigned: 2026-10-16 16:06 +07 | Status: completed | Rationale: one of three mob reviewers (codex gpt-6-astra, muse muse-wonder-1.0, glm glm-5.3); reviewed Fable's 6d plan v2 against origin/master@c6bc91f7 read-only; final message promoted verbatim below by the orchestrator (two CLI startup-warning lines dropped)\n\nMOB-VERDICT: IMPLEMENTATION-READY\n\n# Mob consensus review — slice 6d plan v2 (any-pc snapshots), reviewer glm\n\nScope honored: read-only; Read was my only tool; no git, no Bash, no edits. Tree: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-review-6dv2` (origin/master @ c6bc91f7 with module-pins commit 2 — `check-modules!` confirmed present at `rows.cljc:1914-1940`, so 6d-a's stated gating condition \"module-pins commit 2 on master\" is satisfied in this tree).\n\n## 1. The nine findings\n\n- **F1** (ready queue vs any-pc): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.1 `:ready` row, §3.2, §3.9 item 3(c))** — kernel order verified (`run-loop` active-first `engine.cljc:2211`/queue `:2216`; queue entries are the wait entry plus `:value :status :resource-updates :cursor` minus `:stream`, `engine.cljc:443-462`); resume reads `:resource-updates`/`:value` only, never the entry's `:cursor` (`engine.cljc:2299-2312`) — one mechanism defect inside the row's cell encoding found (my N1 below).\n- **F2** (R0 rejects a scheduler-constructed state): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.1 kind table, §3.2 lift order, item 3(b))** — `resume-from-run-queue` clears flags and leaves `:wait-set` untouched (`engine.cljc:2305-2309`), `register-restore` sets control without touching waits (`semantic.cljc:110-118`); v2's lift order (running → parked → blocked(waits∪ready) → halted) preserves today's wait-first order (`rows.cljc:1686-1691`), so nothing admitted today is demoted.\n- **F3** (fuel bounds neither child nor tick): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.4)** — `(vm/run (stamp-gate …))` at `engine.cljc:1475`, the `:else → phase :running` arm at `:1490-1491`, the `:exporting/:ended` skip at `:1474` (so a budgeted round inheriting `advance-install` keeps the gate check), host seams verified (`semantic.cljc:193`, `engine.cljc:480`, `ffi.cljc:334-350`); `poll-links` is inside `check-wait-set` (`engine.cljc:2168`), so the `scheduler-round-fuel` sketch does not drop link polling.\n- **F4** (tenure loss discards the computed boundary): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.3 four outcomes)** — `run-cycle` verified: tenure `:2124`, `m1` `:2126-2128`, recheck `:2129`, assoc `:2145`; the catch ends the run on the argument state (`driver.cljc:2255-2262`); `exit-mint` requires tenure (`:1148`), the abort path checks live tenure (`:2870-2889`), recovery is the complete fenced snapshot only (`rebuilt-cell` `:2448-2491`, `reopen-source` `:2494-2520`).\n- **F5** (all-pc stack equation false): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.5)** — every kernel citation exact: `stack-layouts` writes only parse-node pcs (`layout.cljc:118-131`), `:jump` is metadata not a child (`code.cljc:389-396`), terminators excluded (`code.cljc:413-414`), and the stack kernel behaves as claimed (`:const` pushes `:689-690`, `:push` only advances pc `:722-723`, tail call keeps `stack'` `:731`/`:747` without a frame `:737-738`, `:jump` `:777-778`, `:branch-false` pops `:780-784`, `:return`/`:halt` peek `:775`/`:787`); emitter order (`derive.cljc:92-97`) and allocator intervals over `live ∪ def` with `end < pc` expiry (`derive.cljc:127-130`, `:140-143`) verified.\n- **F6** (grant-check instruction wrong): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.1 custody table, `check-custody!` unchanged)** — the dispatch is exactly `(when-not (or fenced? (= :halted kind)) (accept-grant! …))` (`rows.cljc:2087`); kind admission lives in `task-ops`/`custody-baseline` (`:1276`, `:1312`).\n- **F7** (refusal predicate broader than L(pc)): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.6 independent oracle, §3.2 lower-defect extension, decision 1)** — `lower-defect` today reads only slot-13 waits positionally and parked states (`layout.cljc:947-950`), `state-prim`/`frame-prim` cover window + lexical + K (`:863-874`, `:853-860`); separate slots 17/18 remove the misread class by construction.\n- **F8** (harness order and shared worlds): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.6)** — order is lift → lower → immediate relift/law 2 → law 6 → run/law 1, isolated worlds, coverage classes; the transport law text confirmed at `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:929+` (\"Canonical round trip\", after the fenced-lower amendment).\n- **F9** (doc text overstates; inventory incomplete): **ANSWERED (v2 §3.7 + §3.8)** — every sampled site exists as quoted: UCF `:17`, quiescence `:762-765`, conformance `:799-806`, totality/`not-at-safepoint` bullet `:810-821`, breakpoint sentence `:823-825`; continuation.md custody prose `:366-371`, contexts `:432-435`, refusal rows `:1248-1252`, second conformance text `:2419-2425`; `yin.vm.semantic.md:680-689` (explicit park \"no-wait safepoint\", quiescence sentence); v2-amendment `:560-570`; ucf-revisions `:602-603`/`:622`; S1/P1 conflict resolved to one ordered diagnostic (`frame.cljc:96-98`, `:162-164`); `:push` wording fixed against `stack.cljc:722-723` + `correspondence` (`layout.cljc:97-108`). Residual staleness in two tuples.md line ranges (N3 below).\n\n## 2. New defects found in v2\n\n- **N1, P2 — the `:ready` row's cursor-cell mechanism is misdescribed; `cell!` cannot do what §3.2 says.** Evidence: plan §3.1/§3.2 (\"cells-node runs each update through cell! so the census numbers the advanced cell beside its current cell\") vs `rows.cljc:1388-1393` — `cell!` is first-wins per census key (`when-not (contains? (:cells @acc) source) …`), and the file header states the invariant outright: \"one resource reached twice is one cursor cell\" (`rows.cljc:1342-1344`). Running the successor through `cell!` either no-ops (id already registered with the current body — the successor position is then nowhere in the row universe and the ready row's `[:ref :cursor-cell-body]` dangles) or, if the cell was never value-reached, registers the **successor** as the census body (wrong for slot 9, which must carry the current). Note the current body does get into the census via the ready row's own wait (`pending-node`'s `cell-for!`, `rows.cljc:1543-1544`, `:1396-1401`), so the fix is local. Minimal fix: the 6d-a brief must say the successor body is emitted as its **own** `:cursor-cell-body` row paired under the same canonical C (not a second `cell!` registration), add the decode rule that one C may pair with two bodies across the task row and its ready rows, and make restore map the ready row's C through the slot-9 cell map while reading the successor body from the ready row (§3.2's restore sentence already has the right shape). Pin in acceptance items 2 and 3(c) — 3(c) would catch a dropped successor at law 1, but a brief written from §3.2 verbatim burns a review round first.\n- **N2, P3 — §3.2's slot-18 `lower-defect` expression is garbled.** `(state-prim code (first (:slots (first (:slots r)))) [:ready i])` double-dereferences: a cref has no `:slots`. Minimal fix: \"resolve the ready row's wait cref to its wait row and feed that row, exactly as the slot-13 arm feeds `(first (:slots w))`\" (`layout.cljc:947`).\n- **N3, P3 — line-offset model stale in this tree; two tuples.md citations point at the wrong text.** The plan's rule \"shift by about +48 lines after 1883\" fails in both directions: measured in this tree `task-ops` +7, `state-node` +15, `lift-task*` +28, `check-install!` −2, `check-custody!` +48, `restore-task`'s kind arms +59; module-pins' doc edits moved the tuples kind-rules paragraph from the cited `:455-460` to `:473-477` and the round-trip law from `:913-917` to `:929+` (the cited ranges now land in the module-pins ruling and the fenced-lower amendment). Content holds everywhere. Minimal fix: the brief locates rows.cljc/tuples sites by symbol and quoted text, never by the plan's line numbers.\n- **N4, P3 — C3's diagnostic name.** Plan §3.1 says `:body-continuity`; `frame.cljc:126` emits `:pc-body`. One-word fix in the brief and acceptance item 1.\n- **N5, P3 — halted-with-outstanding-wait (queue empty) is unspecified and untested.** Constructible (the F2 recipe run to halt instead of `run-fuel 1`); today's lift already classifies it `:blocked` and drops the halt result (wait-set checked before halted, `rows.cljc:1688-1691`), and v2's table preserves that — so no regression, but §3.1's \"the halt value is dead by the kernel's own order\" justification covers only the queued case (with an empty queue the machine is terminal and the result is observable via `vm/value`). Minimal fix: one sentence in §3.1 (\"halted with outstanding waits and an empty queue classifies `:blocked`; the halt value is dropped as today — pinned\") and one pinning test beside item 3(e).\n\n## 3. The eight §3.10 defaults\n\n1. **Task slots 17 (`run`) and 18 (`ready`), one re-mint — SAFE.** The task row has exactly 17 slots today (`row-table`, `rows.cljc:67-69`) and every slot-13 traversal is positional (`task-ops:1281`, `protect!:1754-1755`, `check-pendings!:1902`, `lower-defect:947`), so separate slots remove F7's misread class by construction and the re-mint coincides with module-pins' (already landed here).\n2. **Fuel as `:fuel`, default nil — SAFE.** Pure opt-in at the verified `run-cycle` site (`driver.cljc:2126-2128`); nothing changes until a composition sets it.\n3. **No auto-arm; `hand-off` explicit; `:yin.k/at-boundary` the signal — SAFE.** `hand-off` (`driver.cljc:1122-1138`) is the composition's act today; the later policy needs only `lease/holding?` and the clock the driver already reads (`:2066-2069`), no VM change.\n4. **No adoption of a retained boundary — SAFE.** Without tenure `exit-mint` refuses (`:1148`) and the abort path ends the run (`:2870-2889`); recovery is the complete fenced snapshot (`rebuilt-cell:2448-2491`), and the documents state the loss in those words.\n5. **Keep Q6 edge-reachability, pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal — SAFE.** A documented conservative-discharge false refusal (`discharge!` refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with obligations, `rows.cljc:1654-1655`) pinned as such; the fix is a separate liveness pass, correctly out of scope.\n6. **Completed `:put` wakes are completed operations — SAFE.** `with-custody` numbers `:put` **waits** only and reads slot 13 (`rows.cljc:2100-2106`, `:1754`), so ready rows are outside both numbering and `protect!` by construction; resume delivers `:V` and never re-appends (`engine.cljc:2299-2312`), and post-expiry re-execution dedups by op-id per UCF §7.4.3.\n7. **`:push` relabelled to its successor — SAFE.** `stack.cljc:722-723` shows `:push` only advances pc (its value is already on the stack) and `correspondence` (`layout.cljc:97-108`) is the exact relabel site; no guest semantics run.\n8. **Holds stay refusals — SAFE.** `unliftable-hold`'s list (`rows.cljc:1356-1369`: `:observe`, `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`, `:link-cursor-not-installed`) matches §3.7.1 step 1 exactly, these are driver seams mid-step, and 6d-b's verify-each-resolves-in-one-cycle with a known-gaps row for any that doesn't keeps the default honest.\n\n## 4. What 6d-a still needs (omissions)\n\n- The N1 census/`cell!` specification correction folded into the brief (the one substantive gap; the grammar itself is unaffected).\n- A pinning test for a **terminal-status** ready row: the sweep queues woken entries carrying waitset diagnostics, `:link-refused` and FFI response-loss statuses (`engine.cljc:2143-2147`, `terminal-resume-outcome` `:2233-2252`), and §3.1 carries `:D` verbatim for exactly that — no acceptance item exercises it (item 3(c) uses a success wake); one law-1 case where the receiver raises identically.\n- N5's sentence + test (halted beside a wait, empty queue).\n- Brief hygiene from N2/N3/N4: resolve crefs explicitly in the slot-18 `lower-defect` arm, locate rows.cljc/tuples.md sites by symbol (offsets unusable), use `:pc-body`.\n- Minor note for the brief: `run-fuel`'s inactive test (`active-continuation?`) and `vm-hot`'s own (`(nil? control) AND (nil? k)`, `semantic.cljc:137`) differ; a quiescent ingress-ready machine takes the `vm-hot` route and lands in the unbudgeted `scheduler-round` — harmless today (nothing to run) but worth one sentence so the budgeted round is not assumed universal.\n\n## 5. Citation sampling (as required: which I checked, whether they held)\n\n60+ distinct citations read in the tree; **all held in content** except the two stale tuples.md line ranges and the plan's rows.cljc offset model (N3) and the C3 name (N4). Checked exactly, among others: engine.cljc 247-248, 258-261, 442-463, 480, 532-533, 1474-1491, 2161-2199, 2205-2219, 2280-2312, 2315-2326, 2352-2363; semantic.cljc 50-55, 101-126, 134-143, 176-181, 193, 331-333; rows.cljc 49-69, 1268-1298, 1301-1324, 1356-1369, 1388-1401, 1523-1562, 1610-1656, 1662-1726, 1732-1765, 1860-1911, 1914-1940, 2071-2112, 2151-2316, 2319-2354; frame.cljc 49-61, 96-98, 117-123, 124-127, 162-173; layout.cljc 97-131, 313-336, 466-540, 715-729, 853-874, 916-953; driver.cljc 74-77, 1122-1148, 2066-2069, 2117-2160, 2246-2262, 2297-2322, 2448-2520, 2862-2891; export.cljc 100-149, 274-282, 387-400; stack.cljc (under `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/`) 682-787; code.cljc 382-417, 644-659; derive.cljc 75-149; ffi.cljc 334-350; ledger.cljc 277; authority/grant.cljc 74-75; docs: UCF 13-21, 754-831; continuation.md 364-371, 430-437, 1246-1255, 2416-2427; semantic.md 678-691; v2-amendment 557-580; ucf-revisions 598-625; tuples.md 452-477, 908-931; safepoint_harness_test.cljc 1048-1109 (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent` at :1057 and the two-callers transport at :1093 exactly as §3.8 disposes). One inferred link, flagged as such: the exact call site of `advance-installs` (inside the `poll-links` path per check-wait-set's docstring at `engine.cljc:2149-2155`; `advance-install` itself and `check-wait-set`'s call of `poll-links` at `:2168` are verified). Not verified: the corpus counts (89/22/61 cases) and the effort figures — no way to count without grep.\n\n**Bottom line:** all nine findings are answered with verifiable evidence; the architecture is sound against the tree; my findings are zero-P0/P1, one P2 (the ready-row cell census mechanism must be rewritten in the brief), and four P3s. 6d-a is implementation-ready under the §3.10 defaults with N1–N5 folded into the brief; the owner's invariant is closed for the holder only when 6d-b lands, as the plan itself states.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
