{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '65,100p' review-inputs/1792141480000-architect-6d-plan-v2-mob-muse.muse-wonder-1-0.findings.md; sed -n '1,126p' review-inputs/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"1. **SAFE** — separate slots remove F7's silent-misread class by construction (every slot-13 traversal stays wait-only), and the forced re-mint coincides with module-pins' re-mint that has already landed on this tree.\n2. **SAFE** — fuel as an opt-in composition knob keeps clocks and time-conversion out of the VM while making preemption available; the invariant closes only for compositions that set it, and the plan says so rather than hiding it (subject to N1's bound actually holding).\n3. **SAFE** — `hand-off` is already the composition's explicit act in the driver, `:yin.k/at-boundary` supplies the signal, and a later tenure-aware auto-arm policy needs no carrier or VM change.\n4. **SAFE** — an ended holder holds no tenure, so adoption would be new ledger authority; retaining the boundary for inspection only, with the checkpoint-loss sentence written into the documents, is the honest specified outcome F4 demanded.\n5. **SAFE** — the `ghost` refusal is a bounded, documented discharge conservatism (`:yin.k/unsatisfied` at a pc whose segment cannot be discharged), not a scheduler-state incompleteness; removing it is a separate constant-propagation slice and should not gate 6d-a.\n6. **SAFE** — the `:put` already appended under the source's own authority before the snapshot; counting it as a retained write would make the receiver re-append a datom already on the stream, duplicating the effect.\n7. **SAFE** — `debruijn/stack.cljc:722-723` shows `:push` only advances pc with the value already on the stack, so relabelling to the successor runs no guest semantics and loses nothing.\n8. **SAFE** — holds are driver-internal observations mid-step, not task states at rest, and the default as stated carries its own verification duty (6d-b proves each resolves within one cycle without guest execution, or files a known-gaps row).\n\n## (5) What 6d-a needs that the plan omits\n\n- **O1 — Re-base §3.8 before writing the brief.** The line-granular inventory and every `file:line` in §3.7/§3.9 were read at `7c4dff32`; on this tree rows.cljc is already ≈ +28 lines (e.g. the ready-queue refusal is at `:1680`, `check-custody!` at `:2071`, the kind rules in tuples moved behind the module-pins ruling insert). 6d-a's first task must be re-running the `grep safepoint` inventory and re-pinning all references on post-module-pins master, or implementers will edit by stale line numbers.\n- **O2 — The two P1 fixes folded into 6d-a's scope:** the bounded bridge loop for `run-fuel` (N1, with its FFI-loop acceptance case) and the `vm-hot` gate guard moved from 6d-b (N3, with the fenced-running-step refusal pinned in 6d-a). Without O2, 6d-a ships a preemption API whose bound is false for bridge-served machines and a fenced running machine that can be stepped.\n- **O3 — A decision on the blocked+ready harness seam** (N4): strike the class from the §3.6 driver coverage list (pinning it via items 3d/6 only) or specify the round-phase seam the harness will use; the acceptance text must not assert a class its own driver cannot reach.\n- **O4 — The shared-cursor ready case and the §3.2 sentence correction** (N2): two waiters on one cursor, snapshot after the sweep before any resume, law 1 + law 2 — this is the one ready-carrier aliasing case the F1/F2 acceptance cases (distinct callers/streams) do not exercise.\n- **O5 — A named op-id form check for ready `:put` pendings** (N5) with one malformed-row refusal test, so the §3.1 claim is implemented where the brief says it is.\n\nSummary of the seat's position: v2 answers eight of the nine findings completely and F3 in all but one seam; the grammar, product state model, validator, custody, expiry, stack-layout, and harness-order work is sound and well-evidenced (N2 and the halted-beside-queue rule were checked and are correct). It is NOT-READY as literally specified because N1 leaves `run-fuel`'s central bound false and N3 leaves a steppable exporting machine exposed for the whole of 6d-a — both are one-paragraph amendments to §3.4/§3.9 plus one moved guard, after which this seat would return IMPLEMENTATION-READY without further design review.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-16 07:40:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-16 14:40 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1)\nSession-ID: not exposed to this session (stdout log: collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.fable.stdout.log)\n\n# Slice 6d plan v2: answer to the second architect's review, and a complete replacement plan\n\nRole: Architect. Worktree `datomworld-6d-review` = `origin/master` at `7c4dff32` (the reviewer's HEAD). Inputs: the second review (`collab/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md`, \"the review\"), my v1 plan (`collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md`, \"v1\"), the 2026-10-13 assessment (`collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md`). Every `file:line` below was re-read in this tree today. **What I ran:** one throwaway JVM test namespace (`test/yin/vm/semantic/probe_6d_test.cljc`, `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic.probe-6d-test`, 3 tests, 9 assertions, 0 failures) that reproduces the review's F1, F2 and F5 counterexamples on the real kernel and layouts; it is deleted and `git status` shows only `collab/`. Nothing else was executed; no code or design doc was edited. The owner's invariant (preemptive migration at any pc is primary) is settled and is the premise throughout. Vocabulary per UCF §7.5.3 (cursor / cursor cell / cursor-ref / cell).\n\n**Verdict on the review: all nine findings ACCEPTED.** I dispute nothing of substance. Three were reproduced by execution (F1, F2, F5), six by reading the cited transitions. v1's `PLAN-READY` was unsupported. Section 1 gives the evidence per finding; section 2 lists what v1 got wrong; section 3 is plan v2, a complete replacement.\n\n**Summary for the owner.**\n\n1. **The task is a product, not a sum.** The scheduler constructs a machine with active control **and** remaining ordered waits (`resume-from-run-queue` leaves `:wait-set` untouched, `engine.cljc:2305-2312`; `register-restore` sets control, `semantic.cljc:116-118`), and with active control **and** a queued completed wake (`run-loop` steps the active activation before draining the queue, `engine.cljc:2211`, `:2216`). Both reproduced (§1 F1, F2). The root is therefore `(kind, activation?, ordered waits, ordered ready outcomes, parked table, installs, store …)`. v1's R0 (\"a wait set beside control is malformed\") and the ready-queue refusal are withdrawn. A `:ready` row carries a completed wake (its value, status and cursor update); `:yin.k/not-quiescent` dies.\n2. **Fuel must reach children.** `scheduler-round` runs every install child with `vm/run` to a park or halt (`engine.cljc:1475`). `run-fuel` gives each task of the tree at most `n` instructions per program tick; a child stopped mid-body stays at phase `:running` (the arm already exists, `:1490-1491`) and is carried as a `:running` child task. The guarantee is cooperative: interruption between completed instructions, no wall-clock bound for one instruction or one host seam (`(apply f args)` at `semantic.cljc:193`, `vm/create-stream!` at `engine.cljc:480`, a bridge step, one sweep).\n3. **Tenure loss keeps the boundary, but adopts nothing.** `run-cycle` computes `m1` and then rechecks tenure before `m1` reaches the state (`driver.cljc:2120-2123`, `:2139`); the catch ends the run on the *pre-slice* state (`:2252-2253`). 6d-b assoc's `m1` before every later check so the ended holder retains the newest pure state as `:yin.k/retained-boundary` (inspection only). Expiry recovery stays the last durable checkpoint; **progress since it is lost**, the documents say so, and the mitigation is the composition's `hand-off` before the bound. Adoption of a retained boundary by a regrant is a ledger rule the owner may add later (decision 4); not in 6d.\n4. **The stack-layout equation was false.** `stack-layouts` assigns only parse-node pcs (`layout.cljc:124`); the conditional's `:jump` is `:jump` metadata, not a child (`code.cljc:389-396`), and terminators are outside the expression (`:413-414`). Reproduced: at the jump pc `L = #{0}`, layout `nil`, uses `#{}`. v2 defines `stack-layout(jump) = stack-layout(if) ++ [rd(if)]`, `[]` at terminators, an **ordered** check (`layout(p) ++ uses(p)` in emitter order against the kernel's region), and the dead-prefix rule for tail residuals (the kernel keeps `stack'` on a tail call, `stack.cljc:731`, `:747`). The register pairing argument stands (the review agrees).\n5. **Six corrections to v1's briefs**: keep the halted grant exemption (`rows.cljc:2039` is right as written); the register-target refusal oracle is over the whole payload, not `L(pc)`; harness order is lift, lower, immediate relift (law 2), then run (law 1) in an isolated resource world; a `run` row with a pending is S1 `:frame-shape`, not P1 `:pending-presence`; `:push` is canonicalised to its successor (no guest semantics run), not refused; the amended text separates representability, portability, satisfaction and export readiness.\n6. **Module-pins commit 2 is not conflict-free with 6d** (v1 said it was): it edits `discharge!`/`install-node`/`lift-task*`'s `requires` (`rows.cljc` hunks at 1603-1700), replaces `check-install-admission!` with `check-modules!` (1883-1918) and `lower-task` (2273-2304), and re-mints `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (1,182 lines) and `ledger-v2.txt`. Since it is merging now, **6d-a branches from master after it lands** and re-mints once (the new task slots, decision 1). Line numbers in this report are master `7c4dff32`; they shift by about +48 lines after 1883 once commit 2 is in.\n7. **Order**: 6d-a (grammar, validator, lift/lower, `run-fuel`, the jump-layout fix and the static checks, the reference-lane stepping harness) → then 6d-b (holder, custody, expiry, recovery, Stage E) **and** 6e (layouts' running mode, ordered stack check, `:push` canonicalisation, tail residuals, laws 4/5 on aligned boundaries) in parallel on two branches; docs with each slice. Nothing of 6d runs in parallel with module-pins' merge except writing the 6d-a brief.\n8. **Effort**: 6d-a 3 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 4.5 days; 6d-b 2.5 + 1.5 = 4; 6e 3.5 + 1.5 = 5; docs 1.5 → **15 days**, up from v1's 11; no runtime measurement supports any wall-time forecast. Routing: codex (gpt-6.1-sol) implements, Fable reviews, glm/cmd as non-author fallback (owner 2026-10-15).\n9. **Owner decisions** (§3.10, defaults stated so 6d-a can start): new task slots 17/18 (recommended) vs. a mixed slot 13; fuel as a holder config key, default nil; explicit `hand-off` only, no auto-arm in 6d-b; no adoption of a retained boundary; keep edge-reachability liveness (Q6) and pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal; completed `:put` wakes are completed operations, not retained writes; canonicalise `:push`; holds stay refusals as documented driver transients.\n10. **Readiness**: with the defaults, **6d-a is implementation-ready the moment module-pins commit 2 is on master**, and its brief can be written now. 6d-b and 6e are implementation-ready as specified here but depend on 6d-a's grammar. One acceptance item is a check, not an open design: whether the lane fragment contains a tail call with a non-empty ambient layout (6e item 6 settles it either way).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The nine findings, verified\n\nLegend: **ACCEPTED** = the counterexample or citation holds as stated; evidence is what I read or ran. Probe lines are quoted from the JVM run (`summary` prints `:control :blocked? :halted? :waits :wait-reasons :ready`).\n\n### F1 — ready-queue exception contradicts any-pc migration: ACCEPTED (reproduced)\n\nReading: `run-loop` (`engine.cljc:2205-2219`) tests `(active? v)` first (`:2211`, `active-continuation?` = not blocked and not halted, `:258-261`) and reaches `(seq q)` only after the activation parks or halts (`:2216`). `vm-hot`'s active path never touches `:ready-queue` (`semantic.cljc:139-268`). `check-wait-set` queues **every** woken entry in one call (`:2197-2199`). The transport preserves ordered multiple waits (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:458-460`) and the harness transports `two-callers` (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1093-1107`).\n\nProbe (two-callers, both responses appended, `sweep`, `resume-from-run-queue`, `vm-hot 1`, `lift-task`, `vm/run`):\n\n```\nF1 after sweep:                 {:control nil, :blocked? true, :halted? false, :waits 0, :ready 2}\nF1 after resume-from-run-queue: {:control {:segment -1, :pc 3}, :blocked? false, :halted? false, :waits 0, :ready 1}\nF1 after vm-hot 1:              {:control {:segment -1, :pc 5}, :blocked? false, :halted? false, :waits 0, :ready 1}\nF1 lift refusal:                #:yin.k{:status :yin.k/not-quiescent, :ready 1}\nF1 after vm/run:                {:control nil, :blocked? true, :halted? false, :waits 2, :wait-reasons [:next :next], :ready 0}\nF1 queued entry keys:           (:call-id :cursor :cursor-ref :deliver :env :k :pc :reason :resource-updates :segment :status :stream-id :value :window)\n```\n\nSo an active activation beside a queued completed wake is a real kernel state; one instruction leaves the queue untouched; today's lift refuses it; only running the activation to its next park drains it, which for a pure recursion is unbounded. v1 §2.5 (\"retry after the next cycle\") was not a bounded normalisation. The queued entry is the wait entry plus `:value :status :resource-updates :cursor` (`make-woken-run-queue-entries`, `:442-463`); the `:put` has appended and the `:next` has consumed its element, so it can be neither re-pended nor dropped. **Right carrier**: the task-state product of §3.1 with an ordered `:ready` row list; no normalisation proof exists without executing guest code, so none is claimed.\n\n### F2 — R0 rejects a state the scheduler constructs: ACCEPTED (reproduced)\n\nReading: `check-wait-set` keeps unresolved waits (`:2192-2196`); `resume-from-run-queue` pops one entry, clears `:blocked?`/`:halted?` and **does not touch `:wait-set`** (`:2305-2309`); `register-restore` sets control through `put-registers` (`semantic.cljc:116-118`, `:50-55`). Explicit `:resume` also restores into the existing machine (`engine.cljc:2360-2363`).\n\nProbe (two-callers, only the first response appended):\n\n```\nF2 admitted two-waiter machine: {:control nil, :blocked? true, :waits 2, :wait-reasons [:next :next], :ready 0}\nF2 after check-wait-set:        {:control nil, :blocked? true, :waits 1, :wait-reasons [:next], :ready 1}\nF2 after resume-from-run-queue: {:control {:segment -1, :pc 3}, :blocked? false, :waits 1, :wait-reasons [:next], :ready 0}\nF2 lift of control+wait today:  #:yin.k{:status :yin.k/not-at-safepoint, :pc 3}\n```\n\nActive control plus a remaining wait, no queue needed. The two-waiter carrier is admitted today (lift, lower and transport of it are tested), so v1's R0 would have refused an admitted state as \"inconsistent\". Withdrawn. Kind classification (root) is separated from activation shape (the `:run` row) in §3.1.\n\n### F3 — fuel does not bound an install child or the program tick: ACCEPTED\n\n`scheduler-round` (`engine.cljc:2324-2326`) → `check-wait-set` → `poll-links` (`:2168`) → `advance-installs` → `advance-install` → `(vm/run (stamp-gate (:vm inst) mode))` (`:1475`): the child runs to its own park or halt inside the round, whatever the root's fuel. v1's `run-fuel` fell into an unbounded round whenever the root was inactive. The lower admits only a `:blocked` child (`rows.cljc:1862-1863`) although the lift admits wire phase `:running` (`:1640`, today a synthetic phase, continuation design `:1202`). Also confirmed: `:call` of a host function is one synchronous `(apply f args)` (`semantic.cljc:193`), stream construction calls the composition's constructor (`engine.cljc:480`), `ffi/maybe-run` loops bridge steps on a blocked machine (`ffi.cljc:334-348`). Fuel counts instructions, never time. §3.4 gives the propagation, the child carrier, and the honest guarantee.\n\n### F4 — a tenure failure loses the just-computed boundary: ACCEPTED\n\n`run-cycle` (`driver.cljc:2111-2144`): `require-tenure!` (`:2118`), `m1` (`:2120-2122`), `require-tenure!` (`:2123`) **before** `(assoc state :machine m4)` (`:2139`); `require-tenure!` throws `::cause :lease-bound` (`:2060-2063`); `step-active` catches and calls `(end-run state …)` on the argument, which still holds `m0` (`:2252-2253`). Later cuts: replay/emit/read call `require-tenure!` through `request!` and the observe seam (`:2127-2137`); `binding-check` ends the run before the cycle (`:1991`, `:2013`); a renewal that lands past the bound ends it (`:1810-1811`); `exit-mint` requires tenure (`:1144`); `abort` requires live tenure and proof no offer was accepted (`export.cljc:609-622`), and the driver's abort path ends the run when tenure is gone (`driver.cljc:2868-2887`). Recovery rebuilds only a complete fenced snapshot (`rebuilt-cell`, `:2442-2484`; `reopen-source`, `:2487-2513`). v1 §2.2's sentence (\"tenure dying between slices ends the run with the machine at a boundary, and the holder … can arm the exit before the bound\") conflated two cases and specified neither. §3.3 gives the lifecycle.\n\n### F5 — the all-pc stack equation is false: ACCEPTED (reproduced)\n\n`stack-layouts` walks `(:expr body)` and `:children`, writing `(assoc out (:pc node) pending)` only (`layout.cljc:118-131`); the parser's conditional node is `{:op :if :pc branch-pc :jump jpc :end …}` with the jump as metadata (`code.cljc:389-396`); `parse-body` parses `[start, term)` so the terminator is no node (`:413-414`). The stack kernel: `:const` pushes (`stack.cljc:689-690`), `:jump` only sets pc (`:777-778`), `:branch-false` pops the test (`:780-784`), `:halt`/`:return` peek the top (`:775`, `:787`). Probe over `layout/segment` of two projected programs (the equation column is `(set layout) ∪ uses = L`):\n\n```\nF5 bare (if true 10 20):\n    3 [:jump 5]            L=#{0}    layout=nil  uses=#{}   eq=false\n    5 [:halt 0]            L=#{0}    layout=nil  uses=#{0}  eq=true\nF5 operand position ((fn [x] x) (if true 10 20)):\n    1 [:const 3 true]      L=#{1}    layout=[1]  uses=#{}   eq=true\n    4 [:jump 6]            L=#{1 2}  layout=nil  uses=#{}   eq=false\n    6 [:call 0 1 [2] false] L=#{1 2} layout=[]   uses=#{1 2} eq=true\n```\n\nAt the jump the kernel holds the consequent's value (register 2 in the second program, 0 in the first), which both arms write (`derive.cljc:81-85` emits `then`, `[:jump end]`, `else`). Correct layouts: `stack-layout(jump) = stack-layout(if-pc) ++ [rd(if)]`; terminators `[]` (the equation then holds through `uses`). Set equality also loses duplicate operands; the check must be ordered. **Tail residuals**: a tail `:call` keeps `stack'` (`stack.cljc:731`, `:747`) and pushes no frame (`:737-738`), so the ambient layout of a tail call in operand position (admitted as layout syntax, continuation design `:805-810`; the tail flag is the AST's, `linearize.cljc:116`, judged only as a boolean by item 10, `code.cljc:644-659`) survives **below the callee**, in the current activation's region and in any later frame's slice; `slice-window` today refuses a count mismatch (`layout.cljc:505-506`). Lowering at the tail pc alone does not establish the next body-start boundary. §3.5 gives the dead-prefix rule. The register argument (`derive.cljc:127-130`, `:140-143`) stands.\n\n### F6 — grant-check instruction wrong: ACCEPTED\n\n`check-custody!` (`rows.cljc:2023-2039`): `(when-not (or fenced? (= :halted kind)) (accept-grant! …))`. A `:running` root is not halted, so it already takes `accept-grant!` once `task-ops` (`:1261`) and `custody-baseline` (`:1297`) admit the kind. v1 §4.2 item 4 (\"drop `(= :halted kind)`\") would have demanded a grant for an origin-only result, contradicting v1 §2.1 and the tuples' custody table (`:470`). Corrected brief: preserve the dispatch; extend the kind sets; pin \"a halted exclusive root restores without a grant, gated `:ended`\" as a regression.\n\n### F7 — the primitive refusal predicate is broader than L(pc): ACCEPTED\n\n`lower-defect` (`layout.cljc:916-953`) checks every wait's state through `state-prim` (`:947-948`) and every parked state (`:949-950`); `state-prim` checks the window **and** lexical bindings of the frame and of every `K` frame (`frame-prim`, `:853-860`; `:863-874`). The harness already refuses primitives in a lexical binding, a capture and a nested collection (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1931-1960`). So \"refusal count = pcs with a primitive in `L(pc)`\" undercounts whenever a primitive sits in `E` or in a saved `K` window. Also confirmed: `(state-prim code (first (:slots w)) …)` reads a **wait row's** first slot; fed a `:run` row directly it would read the segment address. §3.6 gives the oracle; the obstruction list (image collision, installs, free-env, retained FFI, registers) is kept whole.\n\n### F8 — harness order compares the wrong state and mutates its fixture: ACCEPTED\n\nv1 §4.1 item 4 said lift → lower → `vm/run` to the end → law 1 → relift → law 2: the relift is of a halted root, not the running one. The transport law says \"no execution between lower and lift\" (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:913-917`). The existing fixtures operate real toy streams, so running a receiver to completion on the stepping source's world appends effects and consumes scripted outcomes. §3.6 corrects the order and isolates worlds, and defines \"every pc\" as every boundary of the chosen executions, pinned by `(segment, pc, structural state)`.\n\n### F9 — the document text overstates and the inventory is incomplete: ACCEPTED\n\nVerified each cited site: `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:368-371` and `:403-408` (custody prose lists three kinds), `:433-435` (`frame/defect` contexts), `:1249-1252` (refusal rows naming the stepped-kernel refusal), `:2419-2425` (a second normative conformance text), `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:455-460` (kind rules), `:466-473` (custody table), `:797-801` (step 7 contexts), `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:800-805` (conformance runs \"to each safepoint\"), `:1912-1913` (\"no engine change\"), `:2842` (no-layer-collapsing row: hot loop untouched, lift over a blocked machine), `yin.vm.semantic.md:680-689` (explicit park is a no-wait safepoint; halt carries a result). v1 §3.3's safepoint definition (\"carries a delivery record and a pending wait\") was wrong for explicit park and halt; \"Every instruction boundary lifts\" overstated; the `:push` wording (\"no canonical state exists here\") was wrong (`stack.cljc:722-723` only advances pc); S1 vs P1 diagnostics conflicted. §3.7 is the corrected text; §3.8 the inventory, now at line granularity for every file `grep safepoint` reaches.\n\n**On the review's eight answers.** Adopted: `run-fuel` is telemetry-free in the sense \"no per-fuel snapshot\", not \"no telemetry\" (parks and resumes emit, `engine.cljc:2352`, `:2363`); step atomicity does not imply transactional external effects (an append that happened before a host exception is not rolled back); `abort` is conditional (`export.cljc:603-622`); exclusive grants authorise one holder's effects and do not physically prevent a trusted caller from lowering twice; \"one behavioural change\" in v1 hid the scheduler and expiry dependencies; the 11-day estimate was for a narrower design.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What v1 got wrong, plainly\n\n1. **R0.** Asserted a kernel invariant (\"every arm that creates a wait clears control, so control beside a wait is malformed\") that holds for a single-waiter organic machine and fails for admitted multi-waiter carriers resumed by the scheduler. Reproduced.\n2. **Ready queue as a refusal.** Called \"run to the next park\" a bounded crossing. It is unbounded guest execution; the queued wake is a performed effect's result that must be carried.\n3. **Fuel bounds the tick.** False: install children run `vm/run` inside the sweep; host seams are atomic and unbounded by fuel.\n4. **Tenure between slices.** Did not read `step-active`'s catch: the computed boundary is discarded, and expiry/recovery semantics were unspecified.\n5. **Stack equation at every pc.** False at jump pcs; `stack-layouts` has no entry at jump or terminator pcs; set equality loses duplicates; tail residuals ignored below a running callee.\n6. **Grant-check brief.** Would have broken halted restoration.\n7. **Refusal allowlist.** Too narrow (lexical and saved-frame primitives) and the `state-prim` call site would have mis-read a `:run` row.\n8. **Harness order and worlds.** Compared a halted relift to a running lift; shared mutable toy streams across points.\n9. **Document text.** Overstated liftability; wrong safepoint definition; wrong `:push` claim; S1/P1 conflict; six sites missing from the inventory; `yang.antlr.md` footnote aside, nothing else of the inventory was wrong, only incomplete.\n10. **Module-pins independence.** Claimed no textual conflict; commit 2 touches `lift-task*`, the install node, `check-install-admission!` → `check-modules!` and `lower-task`, and re-mints every fixture.\n11. **Effort.** Omitted the product carrier, running children, expiry, control-flow layouts and the oracle.\n\nWhat v1 got right and keeps (the review concurs): `:run` ≠ `:act` at one pc; body-start exception to C2/C3; nil-valued keys retained; whole root store, every parked record, source AST associations, fresh receiver seals; existing admission stages; four-key custody arm for a running root; gate `:running` on restore; the kernel-entry gate guard for `:exporting`/`:ended`; fencing before publication; fuel as a composition knob; reference-lane / cross-engine separation; no new global, callback, clock or shared mutable state.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plan v2\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
