{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '127,268p' review-inputs/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; sed -n '50,63p' review-inputs/1792141480000-architect-6d-plan-v2-mob-muse.muse-wonder-1-0.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"### 3.1 State model: the task-state product\n\n**Task** (one machine of the tree, root or install child):\n\n```\ntask = ( kind,                      ; :running | :blocked | :parked | :halted\n         run?,                      ; the active activation ⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩, iff kind = :running\n         waits  = [wait …],         ; ordered pending waits (any kind but :halted)\n         ready  = [ready …],        ; ordered completed wakes, scheduler outcomes (any kind but :halted)\n         parked-table, result?, parked-id?,\n         store, module-stores, cursor-cells, free-env, id-counter, requires, code, installs, custody )\n```\n\n**Kind rules** (replace tuples §5.5 / continuation §2.6; the machine-side predicate in the lift, §3.2):\n\n| kind | machine | frames (waits) | run | ready | parked-id | result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `:running` | `:control` set | 0..n | exactly one `:run` | 0..n | none | none |\n| `:parked` | control nil, `:value` a parked record the table holds | 0..n | none | 0..n | names a table key | none |\n| `:blocked` | control nil, not parked, `(or (seq waits) (seq ready))` | 0..n | none | 0..n (waits+ready ≥ 1) | none | none |\n| `:halted` | control nil, `halted-with-empty-queue?`, no waits | 0 | none | 0 | none | one |\n\nA machine with `:halted? true`, a non-parked value and a non-empty queue (one activation of a multi-waiter machine halted while a wake is queued) is kind `:blocked`: `run-loop` resumes the queue before it reads the halt (`:2216` before `:2219`) and the next halt overwrites `:value`, so the halt value is dead by the kernel's own order; restore gives it `:blocked? true :halted? false`, which `run-loop` treats identically (no waits wake; the queue pops). Law 1 and law 2 hold on it and the harness pins the case (§3.9 6d-a item 6).\n\n**Row grammar** (`rows.cljc` `row-table`; two new row kinds, two new task slots):\n\n```\n:run    [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k]]          ; five slots; no deliver, no pending\n:ready  [[:ref :wait] :V :D [:ref :cursor-cells]]              ; the wait it completes, value, status, cursor updates\n:task   [… slot 13 [:refs :wait]  14 :optional-D  15 :optional-V  16 :optional-D   ; unchanged\n           17 [:refs :run]        ; 0 or 1, by kind\n           18 [:refs :ready]]     ; 0..n, in queue order\n```\n\n- `:run`: `pc` is the instruction about to execute (`0 ≤ pc < count v`); the window is exactly `L(pc)` (`analysis/live`), nil present as `[:lit nil]`, sorted on the wire; `K` as for an `:act`.\n- `:ready`: its `[:ref :wait]` is a `:wait` row (state `:act`/`:tail` + pending), judged in context `:wait` exactly as a pending wait; `:V` is the woken `:value` (for a `:put` the appended datom; for a `:next` the element read, or the shaped refusal map; for an FFI response the response map, the entry's `:call-id` riding the pending as today); `:D` is the `:status` keyword, carried verbatim so the receiver's `terminal-resume-outcome` raises exactly as the source would have; `[:ref :cursor-cells]` is the entry's `:resource-updates`, a pairs row of `C → cursor-cell-body` over cells of the task's census (a `:next` wake advances one cell; a `:put` wake has none: empty row). The entry's redundant `:cursor` field is not carried (resume reads `:resource-updates`, `engine.cljc:2299`, `:2307`). Reasons admitted in a ready row: `:next`, `:put` (an FFI response is a `:next` on the response cursor); any other reason in the queue refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :ready-reason` and is pinned (6d-a verifies by reading `poll-links` whether link wakes ever enter the queue; today they restore directly).\n- Why two new slots rather than a mixed slot 13: every slot-13 traversal (`task-ops` `:1263-1277`, `protect!` `:1725-1727`, `check-pendings!` `:1873-1876`, `lower-defect` `:947-948`, restore's entry mapping) reads wait rows positionally; a `:run` or `:ready` row in the same list is exactly F7's silent-misread class. Separate slots leave those traversals untouched and give the new rows their own. Cost: every task row's bytes change, so every pinned root id re-mints once. Module-pins commit 2 re-mints them all anyway and is merging now; 6d-a lands right after and re-mints once more. Decision 1 (§3.10).\n\n**Validator** (`frame.cljc`), in the existing order:\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | `:yin.k/state ∈ #{:act :tail :run}`; a `run` has exactly `:yin.k/state :segment :pc :env :window :k` and no other key (so a `deliver` or `pending` key on a `run` is `:frame-shape`; R3 of v1 folds into S1) | `:frame-shape` |\n| C2 | `pc` an exact integer; `0 < pc < count v` for an `act` and every return frame; `0 ≤ pc < count v` for a `run` (a body-start pc is a legal running state) | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | body continuity `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame; **not** for a `run` | `:body-continuity` |\n| R1 | every `run` window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n| R2 | `(set (keys window)) = (analysis/live a pc)` exactly (`window-defect` parameterised by the wanted set: `saved(pc, rd)` for act and return frames, `live(pc)` for a run; no `:window-self` analogue) | `:window-extra` / `:window-missing` |\n| A1-A6, T1 | act/tail only; skipped for a `run` | unchanged |\n| P1 | context `:wait` requires `pending`; `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running` require it absent; a `run` is admitted in context `:running` only and `:running` admits only a `run`; an `act` offered in context `:running` is `:pending-presence` (a shape question is S1's, a context question P1's: one ordered diagnostic, tested as such) | `:pending-presence` |\n| E1, E2, K1-K3, X1 | unchanged; a `run`'s `E` is judged at its own pc's chain | |\n\n**Custody by kind** (phase 7's tables, `rows.cljc`):\n\n| Site | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `task-ops` `:1261`, `custody-baseline` `:1297` | kind set `#{:running :blocked :parked :halted}` |\n| `task-ops` | a `:ready` row's `:put` is a **completed** operation: its op-id is checked well-formed (`check-op-id!`) but excluded from `:yin.k/ops` (retained writes) and from `protect!`'s enrolled-target check; a fork may carry it (the write happened under the source's own authority, as a fork's history does). The receiver never re-appends it: resume delivers `:V`. |\n| `header-custody` `:1704-1716` | `:running` takes the four-key arm (a running root has a next observable step; it is a lease subject) |\n| `check-pendings!` `:1877-1882` | the kind table above; `:running` requires `(count run) = 1`, every other kind `0`; `:halted` requires empty waits **and** empty ready |\n| `check-custody!` `:2039` | **unchanged** (F6); `:running` reaches `accept-grant!` through the kind sets |\n| `with-custody` `:2042-2064` | unchanged: numbers retained `:put` **waits** only; gate `:running` for every non-halted root |\n| `check-root-custody!` `:1200-1245` | `:running` validated as blocked/parked |\n| `check-install!` `:1862` | child kind `:blocked` with wire phase `:parked`; child kind `:running` with wire phase `:running`; else `:install-child` |\n| `lower-task` opts | `:grant`/`:protection` required by a blocked, parked **or running** exclusive root |\n\n### 3.2 Lift, lower, restore (`rows.cljc`, `layout.cljc` `state-prim`)\n\n**Lift** (`lift-task*` `:1650-1698`), new step 1-2:\n\n1. **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on `unliftable-hold` as today. (The queue refusal at `:1652-1653` is deleted; `:yin.k/not-quiescent` leaves the outcome vocabulary; §3.7 says why holds stay.)\n2. **Kind.** `:running` when `:control` is set; else `:parked` when the value is a parked record the table holds; else `:blocked` when waits or ready are non-empty; else `:halted` when `halted-with-empty-queue?`; anything else (no control, no waits, no queue, not halted) is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` (unreachable by construction; keep the arm).\n3. **Nodes.** `run` = `{:segment (address! seg) :pc pc :env (:env m) :window (select-keys (:window m) (analysis/live a pc)) :k (or (:k m) [])}` through `state-node` (no `:site`, no `:deliver`) and `context!` for discharge (`live-pcs` from `pc`); waits as today (`:1671`); `ready` = for each queue entry, `(node :ready [(node :wait [(state-node ctx e) (pending-node ctx e)]) (lift-value ctx (:value e)) (:status e) (cells-node ctx (:resource-updates e))])`, where `cells-node` runs each update through `cell!` so the census numbers the advanced cell beside its current cell (two positions of one cell id: the task's `:cursor-cells` row holds the current, the ready row the successor; one canonical number per cell id, as the census already aliases shared cells); the task node gets slots 17 and 18.\n4. `validate-frames` judges slot 17 in context `:running` and each slot-18 wait in context `:wait`. The rest of the lift (store, parked, installs, module stores, discharge, requires, `custody-baseline`, emit) is unchanged; the walk is kind-independent, so a `:running` child is lifted by the same function with `role :child`.\n\n**Lower / restore** (`restore-task` `:2253-2268`): a `:running` task restores `:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries`; `:blocked`/`:parked`/`:halted` as today; every kind restores `:ready-queue` from slot 18 in order, each entry rebuilt as `(assoc wait-entry :value V :status D :resource-updates {local-cell-key cell})` with cell ids mapped through the receiver's fresh keys (the mapping `with-custody`/`restore-task` already keeps for `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`). A fenced restore puts the ready list in `:yin.k/recovery-ready` beside `:yin.k/recovery-waits` and leaves `:ready-queue []`. Nothing runs. `vm/run` (or `run-fuel`) then continues: an active root steps from `pc`; an inactive one sweeps, and the first ready entry resumes before any newly woken wait, in carried order.\n\n**`lower-defect`** (`layout.cljc:916-953`): add `(state-prim code run-row [:run])` over slot 17 (window plus lexical bindings plus `K`, as `:act`) and `(state-prim code (first (:slots (first (:slots r)))) [:ready i])` over slot 18; the de Bruijn backends do not carry install children (unchanged refusal `:installs`), so a running **child** is never lowered into a backend.\n\n**`export/kind-of`** (`export.cljc:274-282`): `(some? (:control m)) → :running` first; `header-agrees?` (`:387-399`) already takes the four-key branch for any non-halted kind; `valid-state?` and `recovery-metadata` gain the ready list (zero issue stamps; the ready entries counted and compared on rehydration).\n\n### 3.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**A running root under a lease.** Full custody slot (policy, occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq, origin on a successor); `accept-grant!` on lower; gate `:running` on restore; offerable by `ledger/offer-defect` (`ledger.cljc:277`) and `grant/offer-decision` (`authority/grant.cljc:74-75`) once both sets list `:running`; `protect!` checks its retained `:put` waits as for a blocked root (there may be some: F2). The restored machine's first effect parks under the gate (`handle-put` `engine.cljc:532-533`, `handle-next`, `ffi/put-request` answering `full`), so the driver performs it; unchanged from a parked root.\n\n**Four outcomes, specified separately.**\n\n1. **Preemption under live tenure** (the normal path). `run-cycle` runs `(run-fuel m0 n)` when `:fuel` is set (§3.4); the slice returns a machine parked, halted, or at a boundary. The cycle continues (replay, emit, reader step) exactly as today; `cycle-result` leaves the phase `:running` and adds `:yin.k/at-boundary true` to the status detail when `(:control m4)` is set. The composition calls `hand-off` (`driver.cljc:1118-1134`) when it wants to export: `arm-exit` → `export/enter` (its `first-refusal` queue check is deleted; holds remain) → the exit steps mint, prepare, encode (`kind :running`), journal the fence, freeze the recovery object, offer. Only a live-tenure holder reaches `exit-mint` (`:1144`). This is the path that closes the owner's invariant for the holder: a long pure computation is interruptible at every `n` instructions and exportable there.\n2. **Tenure dies after a completed slice** (at `:2123`, inside replay/emit/read, or at the next entry's `binding-check`). 6d-b restructures `run-cycle` so each stage's product is assoc'd into the state **before** the next tenure check (`(-> state (assoc :machine m1) require-tenure! …)`), and `step-active`'s catch ends the run on the state it was handed, which now carries the newest completed pure stage. `end-run` gates the machine `:ended` as today; the ended state additionally carries `:yin.k/retained-boundary {:yin.k/pc … :yin.k/segment … :yin.k/steps-since-checkpoint k}` for inspection and diagnostics. **No adoption**: an ended holder holds no tenure, so `exit-mint` refuses, `abort` refuses `:tenure-ended`/`:tenure-unconfirmed`, and the retained bytes are never offered or resumed. Why retention is safe: under the `:running` gate the slice performs no external observation (effects park; FFI requests are retained for the driver), so `m1` is pure local progress; retaining it loses nothing and discloses nothing.\n3. **Expiry and recovery.** The occurrence is regrantable from its **last durable checkpoint**: the pack this holder lowered, or the last successor it published. The successor relowers it (`step-validating` `:2291-2316`, awaiting-grant for a running root as for a blocked one), `accept-grant!` checks the input prefix and `next-op-seq`, and the writer replays retained writes idempotently by op-id (UCF §7.4.3). **Progress since that checkpoint is lost**: pure computation, and effects performed since (they are durable on the streams and will be re-performed only if their op-ids say they were not). Fuel does not repair this; only exporting before the bound does (outcome 1). The documents say so in those words (§3.7).\n4. **Reopen** (`rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-source`): a fenced snapshot of a running root rehydrates with `:control` set and gate `:exporting` (`rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`); `rehydrate-fenced` re-prepares and must reproduce the pack byte for byte (a `:run` frame is a pure function of the restored fields; the ready list likewise). The kernel refuses to step it: `vm-hot` gains the entry guard `(when (contains? #{:exporting :ended} (vm/gate-mode machine)) (fail \"Gate is closed\" …))` before the loop (`semantic.cljc:137`), one map read per call, not per instruction; `run-fuel` inherits it. Today the guard is moot for parked roots (control nil → `scheduler-round`, which observes nothing under a gate); for a running root it is what makes \"the exporting machine is pure data\" (UCF §7.7.4) true.\n\n**Auto-arm before the bound** (decision 3): recommendation is **not in 6d-b**. `hand-off` stays the composition's explicit act (UCF §7.8); `:yin.k/at-boundary` is the signal. A later policy \"arm the exit when remaining tenure is below one renewal interval\" needs only `lease/holding?` and the clock the driver already reads (`:2060-2063`), no clock-to-instruction conversion, and can live in `compose` without touching the carrier.\n\n**Tests (6d-b)**: clock-controlled cuts (a) immediately before the slice, (b) immediately after it (the `:2123` check), (c) in replay, in emit and in the reader step, (d) between two slices at the next entry's `binding-check`, (e) during `exit-mint`/`prepare`/`offer` of a running root; in each: the source is fenced or ended, no effect was performed past the bound, and **either** a durably authorized successor exists (the offer was accepted under live tenure) **or** the recovery checkpoint is the last pack and `:yin.k/retained-boundary` reports the lost steps; byte-equality of `rehydrate-fenced` for a running root with waits and with a ready entry; two competing candidates for a `:running` offer; stale-source stepping refused by the gate guard; `abort` with uncertain offer refused; expired-grant admission refused.\n\n### 3.4 Fuel and budget propagation\n\n**`yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`**, public, `n` a positive integer:\n\n```\n(ffi/maybe-run machine\n  (fn [m]\n    (if (engine/active-continuation? m)\n      (vm-hot m n)                       ; ≤ n instructions of the active activation\n      (scheduler-round-fuel m n))))      ; one sweep, children budgeted, one resume\n```\n\n- `vm-hot m n` already materialises the boundary at exhaustion (`semantic.cljc:142-143`).\n- `scheduler-round-fuel m n` = `check-wait-set` with `advance-installs` taking the budget: each install child is stepped with `(run-fuel child n)` instead of `(vm/run …)` (`engine.cljc:1475`); a child that returns with `:control` set is stored at phase `:running` (the existing `:else` arm, `:1490-1491`); a blocked child `:parked`; a halted child goes to `link-install` as today. Then `resume-from-run-queue` once. **Per-tree bound**: each task of the tree executes at most `n` instructions per program tick, so a tick is at most `n × (tasks in the tree)` instructions plus the sweep's scan plus the host seams listed below. Nested install children are tasks of the tree and get the same `n`.\n- `ffi/maybe-run` (`ffi.cljc:334-348`) loops bridge steps while the machine is blocked and a handler answered; each loop costs another slice; the bridge step itself is a host seam.\n- `vm/run` and `vm/step` are unchanged (`vm/step` keeps its telemetry snapshot; `run-fuel` emits none per slice, but parks and resumes inside it emit as today).\n\n**The honest guarantee** (goes into the documents verbatim): *Fuel counts completed instructions. A holder with fuel `n` is interruptible at the boundary after at most `n` instructions of each task of its tree per program tick. No wall-clock bound is claimed for one instruction: a `:call` of a host function (`semantic.cljc:193`), a stream constructor (`engine.cljc:480`), a bridge step, one scheduler sweep, and a `:var` resolution are atomic host seams whose duration the VM does not control. The VM has no clock and converts no time to instructions; `dao.lease` owns liveness; the composition picks `n` against its renewal interval.*\n\n**Running install children in the carrier**: a child with `:control` set lifts as a `:running` child task (same walk); wire phase `:running` ↔ child kind `:running`; `check-install!` admits it (§3.1); receiver admission per child is unchanged (`check-modules!` after module-pins); a running child restores with its control set and continues in the next sweep; the de Bruijn backends refuse installs as today. Tests (6d-a item 8): a long initializer preempted mid-body, lifted, lowered, continued to `linked`; a nested initializer (child of a child); handoff while a child is running (6d-b).\n\n### 3.5 Stack layouts at control-flow pcs; the register side\n\n**Static layouts** (`layout.cljc` `stack-layouts`, 6d-a): for a parse node `:if` at `b` with jump pc `j`, result register `rd(if)` (the `rd` the arms define; the emitter writes both arms to it, `derive.cljc:81-85` for H, the A linearizer for the register), define `layout(j) = layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]`; for a terminator pc (`:return r`, `:halt r`) define `layout = []`; every other pc as today. Then at every pc inside a body:\n\n```\nordered:  region(p) = layout(p) ++ [W[u] for u in uses-in-order(p)]        ; emitter order (derive.cljc:92-97): :call operator then operands, :ffi-call operands, :stream-put target then value\nset:      (set layout(p)) ∪ (set uses(p)) = L(p)   for every reachable non-tail pc\n          (set uses(p)) = L(p)                     for every reachable tail-call pc\n```\n\nThe ordered form is the check (duplicates `(f x x)` are two positions holding one value; the lift asserts they agree). `jump` and `branch-false` pcs: branch pc keeps the ancestors' layout (`uses` is the test, which H pops); jump pc as above. **`:push` pcs of H**: canonicalised, not refused: `stack-layout` at a `:push` is undefined because it is no A pc; the kernel state at a `:push` equals the state at its successor except pc (`stack.cljc:722-723`); `h->a` maps `hpc` to `h->a(hpc+1)` when `hpc` is a `:push` (never two pushes in a row, `correspondence` `:97-108`); the lift relabels, running nothing; laws 4/5 compare at the aligned A boundary. The design calls this a stutter of the stack backend, not a property of A.\n\n**Dead prefix (tail residuals)**: a tail `:call` consumes `f` and its args and keeps the rest of the region (`stack.cljc:731`, `:747`) without pushing a frame (`:737-738`). Hence inside a body entered by a tail call, and transitively, the current activation's region and any later frame's slice may carry a **dead prefix** below the layout: values the ambient layout syntax pushed, never read again (every H read is top-relative: `:call` by `f-pos` from the top, `:branch-false`/`:return`/`:halt` peek; `return-transition` truncates to `stack-base`, which includes the prefix, and the prefix stays dead). Rule: `lift-stack` takes the **top** `|layout(p)| + |uses(p)|` values of the current region and the top `|layout(pc_i − 1)|` of each frame's slice and drops the rest (`slice-window`'s count equality becomes `≥`, with the excess recorded as `:yin.k/dead-prefix n` in the lift's diagnostics, never in the frame); `lower-stack` produces no prefix and recomputes bases cumulatively (`stack-k` `:635-651` already does). Unobservable: law 1 on continuing to the reference result; law 2/4 since the relift of the lowered kernel has no prefix and the canonical form never had one. **6e item 6** either exhibits a lane-fragment case with a non-empty ambient layout at a tail call (then the rule is exercised) or proves by the static check `layout(p) = [] at every tail-call pc of the fragment` that none exists (then the rule is pinned on a hand-built vector). Both are acceptance, not open design.\n\n**Register side**: `allocation` gives distinct slots to virtuals both in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` (`derive.cljc:127-130`), freeing only when the interval end `< pc` (`:140-143`), so `virt(p, s)` over `L(p)`'s image is single-valued at every pc; `register-window` over `L(p)` for a run; `write-back`-style nil fill outside `L(p)`. Unchanged from v1; the review concurs; it is still a check (§3.9 6d-a item 7).\n\n### 3.6 Harness order, worlds, oracle, coverage\n\n**Order per point** (reference lane, 6d-a; backends, 6e): at boundary `S_i` of the stepping source: (1) **lift**; (2) **lower** into a fresh receiver of an **isolated world** (§ below); (3) **immediate relift** of the receiver and **law 2** (`canonical-under` root equality with the lift; no execution in between); (4) **law 6** on every lifted frame (plus the two window tampers); (5) **run the receiver to the end** in its world, **law 1** (result and effect trace equal the reference's, after `normalize`'s fresh-identity correspondence); (6) negative controls kept: cursor positions and descriptors, windows and lexical values tampered are refused or differ as today.\n\n**Isolated worlds**: each point builds the receiver's composition and streams fresh from the case's **deterministic script** (the same scripted inputs the reference run saw, fed to the receiver's own streams); the stepping source's world is never shared with a receiver; fresh identities normalise through the existing correspondence (`normalize`, `:238`). The reference result and trace are computed once per case on a pristine world.\n\n**\"Every pc\"** means every boundary of the chosen executions (the stepping driver's `S_0 … S_end` for each case and each wrapper), not every CFG-reachable pc nor every possible activation. Coverage is pinned by `(segment-address, pc, structural-class)` where the class is one of: body entry, branch taken, branch untaken, jump, non-tail call, return, tail transition, capture (`:current-continuation`), resume, park, FFI park, scheduler product (active + waits, active + ready, blocked + ready), install child running; the harness asserts each class is reached at least once on the corpus and pins the point count per case per host. The static all-pc checks (§3.5) and the dynamic pass are complementary; neither proves a universal theorem and the docs say so (§3.7 \"claims that are checks\").\n\n**Refusal oracle (6e)**: for each case and point, the expected register-target refusal is computed by an **independent traversal in the test** over the decoded task: any `:prim` row reachable from the `run`'s window or lexical bindings, from any `K` frame's window or bindings, from any wait's or ready's state, or from any parked state. Assert per direction: exactly the expected points refuse `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` before any attach (attach count zero), and **every other point transfers** and continues to the reference result. The other obstructions (image collision, installs, free-env procedure, retained FFI) are asserted on their existing fixtures and stay in the documented limit list.\n\n\n**N1 — P1 — `run-fuel`'s bounded-return contract fails through `ffi/maybe-run`.** Plan §3.4 defines `run-fuel` as `(ffi/maybe-run machine (fn [m] …))` and guarantees (§3.4, honest-guarantee paragraph) interruption \"after at most `n` instructions of each task of its tree per program tick.\" But `maybe-run` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:334-350`) loops — `(recur (run-fn vm))` after every answered bridge step — with no budget, iteration cap, or return to the caller. A bridge-served program that keeps making FFI requests (each iteration individually fuel-bounded, the loop not) never returns from one `run-fuel` call, so the holder never regains control: exactly the unbounded-in-one-tick class F3 required closing, one seam over. The plan's bullet \"each loop costs another slice\" concedes the iterations exist but never bounds them per call. Minimal fix: give `run-fuel` a bounded bridge loop — e.g. return the machine after one bridge cycle and let the holder re-invoke, or thread a call-level slice budget into a `maybe-run` variant — and add an acceptance case (an FFI-looping program preempted by `run-fuel`) to 6d-a item 9.\n\n**N2 — REFUTED as a code defect; the plan's mechanism is right and one of its sentences is wrong (P3 documentation).** My candidate was that the first-wins census (`ucf/rows.cljc:1388-1393`, `cell!` registers only the first body per cell id) would drop a `:ready` row's successor cell. Reading the lift order refutes it: a ready entry's pending registers its cell via `cell-for!` (`:1542-1544`), which reads the machine's own `:resources` — and `check-wait-set` (`engine.cljc:2192-2199`) has already advanced those resources to the final successor before any entry is queued, so every registration of one cell id in one lift reads the identical current body and first-wins aliasing is consistent; carried values, not re-reads, deliver the intermediate elements, in queue order. What is wrong is v2 §3.2's sentence claiming \"the task's `:cursor-cells` row holds the current, the ready row the successor; two positions of one cell id\" — there are not two positions; there is one canonical (final) body, and that is precisely why it works. Minimal fix: amend that sentence in §3.2 to say the ready row references the census's single current cell body, and pin the semantics with a shared-cursor case (two waiters on one cursor, snapshot after the sweep before any resume) in 6d-a acceptance item 3. No code change needed.\n\n**N3 — P1 — The kernel gate guard is sequenced one slice too late.** Plan §3.2 gives 6d-a the restore: a `:running` task restores with `:control` set, and \"a fenced restore puts the ready list in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`… and leaves `:ready-queue []`\" — but nothing strips or parks the control. The existing fenced arm (`ucf/rows.cljc:2109-2111`) moves only waits and parked records into recovery. The `vm-hot` entry guard that makes \"the exporting machine is pure data\" true is assigned to 6d-b (§3.3 outcome 4; §3.9 6d-b item 3). So throughout 6d-a, `lower-task {:fenced true}` on a running root yields a machine with control set and gate `:exporting` that `vm-hot` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:137-143`, no gate check) will happily step — pure computation advances, windows mutate, and rehydrate byte-equality is destroyed. The second review explicitly required kernel fencing to ship with the first exposed running path. Minimal fix: move the one-check `vm-hot` entry guard from 6d-b item 3 into 6d-a (it is a single `contains?` read at entry, outside the instruction loop), and add the \"fenced running machine refuses `vm/step`\" assertion to 6d-a acceptance instead of only 6d-b.\n\n**N4 — P2 — One §3.6 coverage class is unreachable by the driver §3.6 defines.** §3.6 requires the stepping driver to pin coverage by structural class including \"scheduler product (… blocked + ready)\" and asserts \"each class is reached at least once on the corpus\"; §3.9 item 4 defines that driver as `run-fuel 1` boundaries only. But `run-fuel` on a blocked machine performs the entire budgeted round — sweep, budgeted installs, and the resume (§3.4) — so the post-sweep/pre-resume blocked+ready state never materializes as a returned boundary. Item 3(d) in fact constructs that state by calling `check-wait-set` directly, outside the driver. As written, the coverage assertion in §3.6/§5.7 cannot pass. Minimal fix: either pin blocked+ready solely through the products test (items 3d/6) and strike it from the driver's class list, or give the harness an explicit round-phase seam and say so in §3.6. (The companion re-examination — halted-beside-queue reclassified `:blocked` in §3.1 — I checked and it is CORRECT, not a defect: `run-loop` (`engine.cljc:2216-2219`) reads the queue before the halt, a terminal ready entry throws in `resume-from-run-queue` (`:2310-2311`) before any halt value is read, and any resumed activation overwrites the halt when it next parks or halts; the halt value is dead by the kernel's own order.)\n\n**N5 — P3 — The completed-`:put` op-id well-formedness check has no site.** §3.1's `task-ops` bullet says a ready `:put`'s op-id \"is checked well-formed (`check-op-id!`) but excluded from `:yin.k/ops`\". In the current code `check-op-id!` is called from inside `task-ops` itself (`ucf/rows.cljc:1286-1291`); excluding ready rows from `task-ops` removes the only caller, and no other stage in §3.1's validator table is named as performing the check (acceptance item 2 tests row shape and status-keyword, not op-id form). A malformed op-id inside a carried ready `:put` pending would therefore pass lower silently — harmless to the receiver (it delivers `:V`, never re-appends), but the plan's stated check does not exist as specified. Minimal fix: name the site — e.g. `check-pendings!` over slot 18 reuses `check-op-id!` for form only — and add one malformed-op-id ready-row refusal to acceptance item 2.\n\n## (4) The eight §3.10 defaults\n\n(As I treat them: 1. task slots 17 `:run` / 18 `:ready` with a one-time re-mint after module-pins; 2. fuel as holder config `:fuel`, default nil; 3. no automatic export at the bound in 6d-b, explicit `hand-off` only; 4. no adoption of a retained boundary, recovery is the last durable checkpoint; 5. keep edge-reachability liveness (Q6) and pin the pre-branch `ghost` false refusal; 6. completed `:put` wakes are completed operations, excluded from retained writes and `protect!`; 7. `:push` relabelled to its successor, not refused; 8. holds stay refusals as documented driver transients.)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
