{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ve checked the restore consumers and the exception path. Completed outcomes need both a delivery state and a completion descriptor, because link wakes discard request fields that pending-wait validation requires. For tenure loss, I’ll specify stage-local catches that return the latest completed state; rebinding inside `run-cycle` cannot update the outer catch’s argument.\n"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"# Slice 6d plan v2.1: reconciled mob amendments\n\nRole: Architect. Baseline: Fable’s `1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md`. Governing reconciliation: `1792143479000-orchestrator-6d-plan-v2-mob-consensus.md`. Verification tree: `c6bc91f7`, including module-pins commit 2.\n\nThis is an amendment edition: the replacement blocks below supersede the named v2 sections or clauses completely. Unmentioned text remains unchanged. Section numbers are retained. Historical execution claims in v2 remain attributed to Fable; this revision performed source inspection only, ran no tests, and changed no files.\n\nThe owner ratified defaults 1–5 and 7 unchanged, and defaults 6 and 8 with the amendments written into §3.10 below. The three P1 amendments all belong in 6d-a.\n\n**Replacement for the opening summary’s affected claims:**\n\n1. The task remains a product: active control, ordered outstanding waits, ordered completed outcomes, parked records, stores and installs. Completed outcomes cover stream, FFI, link and install wakes, including terminal outcomes. They are not validated as outstanding waits.\n2. `run-fuel` performs one budgeted execution slice or scheduler round, followed by at most one bridge step. It never restarts the runner inside the same call. Child execution receives the same bounded entry point.\n3. The closed-gate guard ships in 6d-a with the first running fenced restore. Direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` paths are covered.\n4. Tenure failure is handled at stage boundaries with an explicit state-bearing outcome. The newest completed machine is retained for inspection, never adopted without authority; recovery remains the last durable checkpoint.\n5. Ready cursor updates carry independently emitted cell bodies under the task census’s canonical cell identities. Completed operation IDs receive shape validation separately from retained-write custody validation.\n6. Module-pins commit 2 has landed. Implementers locate sites by symbol and quoted text, not by v2’s obsolete offset estimate.\n7. Order remains 6d-a, then 6d-b and 6e on separate branches. 6d-a supplies the carrier, bounded runner and fencing; 6d-b supplies holder scheduling and migration lifecycle. The complete holder invariant is not claimed before 6d-b.\n8. The effort figures remain estimates, not deadlines or review-round limits. Acceptance gates determine completion.\n\n**Change:** Incorporates consensus P1 items 1–3, P2 items 4–8, and citation/sequencing hygiene.\n\n## 1. The nine findings, verified\n\n**Replacement for the section’s interpretation of readiness:**\n\nThe nine findings against v1 remain accepted. Fable’s reproduced cases and source observations remain historical evidence. V2’s proposed answers required the reconciled amendments below: completed link/install outcomes, bounded bridge execution, immediate fencing, explicit exception-state transport, correct cursor-update encoding, corrected acceptance equations and complete ready-value traversal.\n\nNo v2 assertion that link wakes restore directly, that unchanged `maybe-run` bounds a fuelled invocation, or that local `assoc` updates an outer catch’s state is normative in v2.1.\n\n**Change:** Preserves the original findings while superseding mechanisms rejected by the mob.\n\n## 2. What v1 got wrong, plainly\n\nRetain v2 §2. Append:\n\n12. **V2’s remaining execution gaps.** The bridge loop could restart the budget, and the gate guard was scheduled after running fenced states became available.\n13. **V2’s completed-outcome gap.** Link and install completions already enter the queue, after their outstanding-operation resources may have been retired.\n14. **V2’s mechanical corrections.** Cursor identities and update bodies require separate treatment; completed operation IDs need shape-only validation; the ready value participates in the portability oracle; stage-local state must cross failure handling explicitly; ordered operand multiplicity is not set cardinality.\n\n**Change:** Records the reconciled amendments without rewriting v1’s history.\n\n## 3. Plan v2.1\n\n### 3.1 State model: the task-state product\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.1 completely.**\n\n**Task**, root or install child:\n\n```text\ntask = (kind,\n        run?,\n        waits = [wait …],\n        ready = [ready …],\n        parked-table, result?, parked-id?,\n        store, module-stores, cursor-cells, free-env, id-counter,\n        requires, code, installs, custody)\n```\n\n| Kind | Machine classification | Waits | Run | Ready | Parked-id | Result |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---|\n| `:running` | `:control` set | 0..n | exactly one | 0..n | absent | absent |\n| `:parked` | control nil; value names a held parked record | 0..n | none | 0..n | table key | absent |\n| `:blocked` | control nil; not parked; waits or ready nonempty | 0..n | none | 0..n | absent | absent |\n| `:halted` | control nil; halted; waits and ready empty | none | none | none | absent | present |\n\nA halted activation beside queued work classifies `:blocked`: the scheduler processes the queue before returning the halt result. A halted activation with outstanding waits and an empty queue also classifies `:blocked`, dropping its halt value as today. The latter is an existing carrier normalization, not a claim that the source halt value is unobservable; acceptance pins it separately.\n\n**Row grammar.** Task slots 0–16 retain their meanings; slots 17 and 18 remain separate ordered references.\n\n```clojure\n:run\n[:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k]]\n\n:ready\n[[:ref :act :tail]          ; delivery state, without an outstanding pending\n [:ref :completion]        ; completed-operation descriptor\n :V                        ; delivered value, including [:lit nil]\n :optional-D               ; nil or status keyword\n [:ref :cursor-cells]      ; resource updates, not another census\n :optional-V]              ; raised refusal data, absent unless required\n\n:completion :completion    ; discriminated by completion-table\n\n:task\n[...                       ; slots 0–16 unchanged\n [:refs :run]              ; slot 17\n [:refs :ready]]           ; slot 18\n```\n\n`:completion` is a new discriminated structural row. Its descriptor table is:\n\n| Completion tag | Slots after the tag | Restored engine meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:next` | `C` | Ordinary completed reader; reconstruct cursor-ref and stream-id through the census. |\n| `:put` | `stream-cref, datom-V, optional-op-id-D` | Ordinary completed append outcome; never append again. |\n| `:ffi` | `C, call-id-D, op-D` | Completed FFI response reader; restore engine reason `:next` and correlation fields. |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D, envelope-V, request-stream-cref, response-stream-cref, C, optional-op-id-D` | Completed request append; restore `:request-sent true`, so successful resume creates the response wait rather than delivering the append result to the guest. |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D, optional-op-id-D` | Completed link-request outcome; no outstanding request or response transport is required. |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D, module-S, try?-D` | Completed link-response outcome. |\n| `:install` | `module-S, try?-D` | Completed initializer outcome; no remaining install entry is required. |\n\nThe existing `pending-table` remains the grammar of **outstanding** operations. `completion-table` is distinct. The `:ffi` and `:ffi-request` wire tags preserve the existing distinction between engine `:next` and `:put` entries carrying FFI correlation.\n\nAll existing queue producers are covered. `wake-installed` queues install completions, and `poll-links` queues link outcomes. A completion must not reconstruct a retired request envelope, response cursor or install solely to satisfy pending validation.\n\nFor link/install success, status may be absent: it is encoded canonically as nil. A try failure travels in `:V` as ordinary returned outcome data. An ordinary require failure carries status `:link-refused` and its refusal value in the last slot; restore reinstates `:refusal` so the existing terminal-resume path raises the same error. The refusal slot is absent for other outcomes. Status is preserved, not inferred from the delivered value.\n\nThe redundant queue-entry `:cursor` is not carried. Its operative resource updates are carried explicitly. Empty or nil update maps have one spelling: an empty update row.\n\n**Frame and completion validation.**\n\n| Rule | Requirement | Diagnostic |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | States are `:act`, `:tail`, `:run`; a run has exactly state, segment, pc, env, window and k. | `:frame-shape` |\n| C2 | Exact integer PC; act and return-frame PCs are positive and in bounds; run PC may be zero. | `:pc-kind`, `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | Act and return frames retain body continuity; the run itself skips that predecessor check. | `:pc-body` |\n| R1 | Run window keys are in the body’s register scope. | `:window-scope` |\n| R2 | Run window keys equal `L(pc)`; act/return windows retain `saved(pc, rd)`. | `:window-extra`, `:window-missing` |\n| A1–A6, T1 | Existing delivery-state rules remain for act/tail; skipped for run. | Existing diagnostics |\n| P1 | `:wait` requires pending; `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running`, `:ready` require pending absent. Run is admitted only in `:running`; `:ready` admits only act/tail. | `:pending-presence` |\n| E1, E2, K1–K3, X1 | Existing environment, continuation and task-context checks remain. | Existing diagnostics |\n| Q1 | A ready completion’s tag must be compatible with the delivery site, using the same site/reason relation as waits. | `:reason-mismatch` |\n| Q2 | Link/install completion `try?` is boolean; completed install does not require membership in the task’s installs. | `:pending-try` |\n| Q3 | Ready status is nil or a keyword; `:link-refused` requires link/install completion, `try? false`, and refusal data; other outcomes have no refusal slot. | `:ready-outcome` |\n| Q4 | Every update key names a cell in this task’s slot-9 census; update rows have unique keys and valid bodies. | Existing missing-cell/duplicate-key diagnostics |\n| Q5 | Every present completed operation ID passes shape-only validation. | `:malformed-op-id`, `:inexact` |\n\nQ1 and Q2 receive the completion descriptor through the validation context; they do not fabricate a pending field on the delivery frame. Ready FFI descriptors contribute their call IDs to task context alongside outstanding FFI waits, so parked FFI records remain valid while their response or sent-request outcome is queued.\n\n**Custody by kind.**\n\n| Site | Required behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `task-ops`, `custody-baseline`, `check-root-custody!` | Admit `:running` beside the existing kinds. |\n| `header-custody` | Running exclusive roots take the existing four-key arm, plus origin when present. |\n| `check-pendings!` | Validate task cardinalities, outstanding waits, installs, and completed descriptors through separate paths. |\n| `check-pendings!`, slot 18 | Invoke a factored `check-op-id-shape!` for every present completed operation ID. |\n| Existing `check-op-id!` | Call that shape helper, then retain origin, occurrence and counter checks for retained writes. |\n| `task-ops`, `protect!`, `with-custody` numbering | Continue to operate on outstanding writes; completed outcomes are excluded. |\n| `check-custody!` | Preserve the fenced/halted grant exemption. |\n| `check-install!` | Admit blocked child/parked phase and running child/running phase. |\n| `lower-task` | Preserve exclusive grant/protection requirements for non-fenced running roots. |\n\n`check-op-id-shape!` checks the existing two-field operation-ID shape, valid occurrence and exact sequence representation. It does not require root origin, compare the historical occurrence to the current root, or impose the current root’s sequence frontier. Nil remains the canonical absence of an ID for ungated operations.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-3; P2-5; glm N4/N5; terminal-status and FFI-context consequences of completed-state validation.\n\n### 3.2 Lift, lower, restore\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.2 completely.**\n\n**Lift, `lift-task*`:**\n\n1. Refuse the existing holds through `unliftable-hold`. Remove the ready-queue refusal.\n2. Classify kind by §3.1, preserving running → parked → blocked → halted precedence.\n3. Build the run from control, environment, K and `select-keys` of the current window over `L(pc)`; register its code/environment context for discharge.\n4. Build outstanding waits through the existing pending path.\n5. Build each ready entry through a separate completion path: delivery-state node, completion descriptor, lifted outcome value, optional status, explicit resource-update row and optional lifted refusal.\n6. Validate run frames in `:running`, ready delivery frames in `:ready`, and their completion descriptors through Q1–Q5. Outstanding waits remain in `:wait`.\n7. Preserve the existing store, parked-table, module-store, dependency discharge, module-pin and child-admission behavior.\n\n**Cursor identity and update-body encoding.**\n\nSlot 9 is the task’s sole cursor-cell census. `cell-for!` registers each referenced resource identity with its **current machine resource body**. On the shared-cursor sweep path, that body is already the final post-sweep body. Do not describe it as a pre-sweep position.\n\nFor each ready resource update:\n\n- Register or resolve the same resource identity through the task census.\n- Emit the update’s own `:cursor-cell-body` node directly from its stream and position.\n- Pair that node with the same canonical C in the ready update row.\n- Do not register the update body through a second `cell!` call.\n\nThus one C may be paired with different bodies across slot 9 and ready rows, or with equal bodies that Merkle sharing coalesces. Neither case creates a second cell identity.\n\nThe codec must distinguish **the census at task slot 9** from **update rows referenced by ready entries**. Existing blanket skips of every `:cursor-cells` row are not sufficient. Audit cell discovery, canonical numbering, emission and decoded validation: skip the census as a source of ordinary identity occurrences, but traverse ready update keys and bodies as structural references. Numbering uses the task census’s identities; update rows never establish a separate census. Missing census membership and duplicate keys refuse before attachment.\n\n**Lower/restore.**\n\nA running task restores:\n\n```clojure\n{:control {:segment local :pc pc}\n :window W\n :env E\n :k (when (seq K) K)\n :halted? false\n :blocked? false\n :value nil\n :wait-set entries}\n```\n\nEvery kind restores slot 18 in order. Resolve the ready state and completion descriptor, reconstruct the engine entry described in §3.1, then restore its value, status, refusal and resource updates. Update keys map through the slot-9 C→fresh-resource-key map; update bodies come from the ready row, not from slot 9.\n\nNo completed operation is polled or reissued by lower. A completed FFI request append resumes through the existing response-wait transition. Link/install completion restores without resurrecting retired pending resources.\n\nA fenced restore retains ready entries in `:yin.k/recovery-ready`, alongside recovery waits and parked records, and exposes an empty scheduler queue. Running control remains available for byte-identical relift, protected by the 6d-a closed-gate guard.\n\n**Backend refusal traversal.**\n\n`lower-defect` inspects the slot-17 run through `state-prim`. For each slot-18 row it explicitly resolves the first cref to its delivery-state row, then calls `state-prim` on that row. It also calls `value-prim` on the outcome `:V` and optional refusal value. Any value-bearing completion fields that will be restored as backend payload receive the same traversal.\n\nThis replaces v2’s double-dereference expression. With the corrected grammar there is no intermediate wait row to dereference.\n\nThe independent test oracle traverses the same semantic roots independently of implementation helpers. Existing image-collision, free-environment, install and retained-FFI backend limits remain. Unsupported ready values must refuse before attachment.\n\n**Export classification.**\n\n`export/kind-of` follows the complete §3.1 precedence, including ready-only blocked tasks. Recovery metadata and validity checks include the ordered ready list. Export/abort/reopen preserve the list rather than executing it.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-4 adopts glm N1’s independent update-body encoding and muse N2’s shared-cursor interpretation; P2-7 and glm N2 fix ready-value traversal and cref resolution.\n\n### 3.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**Replace outcome 2, outcome 4 and the tests paragraph; retain outcomes 1 and 3 and explicit hand-off policy.**\n\n**2. Tenure dies after a completed slice.**\n\n`run-cycle` becomes a sequence of stage-local transitions. A stage receives the latest holder state and returns either a completed successor state or an explicit failure outcome containing the latest completed state.\n\n```text\nstage-result =\n  {:status :ok,     :state completed-state}\n  or\n  {:status :failed, :state latest-completed-state, :failure failure}\n```\n\nEach stage:\n\n1. Checks tenure against its input state.\n2. Executes its operation, retaining the existing tenure checks at external seams.\n3. Associates a successfully returned machine and stage metadata into a successor state.\n4. Passes that successor to the next stage.\n\nThe stage-local catch closes over that stage’s input state. A later tenure failure therefore returns the state containing the previous stage’s completed machine. It does not fall back to `step-active`’s original argument.\n\nThe failure consumer calls `end-run` with the state carried in the failure outcome. `end-run` retains its machine, gates it `:ended`, and records `:yin.k/retained-boundary` diagnostics. Journal-uncertain and inbox-refusal exceptions retain their existing propagation rules; they are not converted into ordinary program failures.\n\nIf an operation throws before returning a machine, retain the last completed stage, not an invented partial result. External effects already performed are not rolled back. No retained boundary receives new execution or publication authority.\n\n**4. Reopen and fencing.**\n\nA fenced running restore keeps control, window, environment and K, with gate `:exporting`. The kernel entry guard is already present from **6d-a**. It refuses `:exporting` and `:ended` before executing an instruction or entering a scheduler/bridge path.\n\nDirect `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel` are covered. The guard remains outside the instruction loop. `rehydrate-fenced` must reproduce the published pack byte for byte, including ordered completed outcomes and cursor updates.\n\n**Tests, 6d-b.**\n\nRetain the five clock-controlled cuts from v2: before the slice, immediately after it, during replay/emit/read, between slices, and during running-root export. At each cut assert the **exact retained machine**, not merely its PC or the presence of diagnostics. A failure inside a stage retains its input machine; a failure after a completed stage retains its output machine.\n\nKeep the source fenced or ended, forbid effects after tenure loss, and verify either an authorized successor or recovery from the last durable checkpoint. Retain competing-candidate, uncertain-abort, expired-grant and recovery-byte-equality cases. Repeat the 6d-a execution-entry fencing checks through the holder’s reopened object.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-8 supplies an explicit exception-state channel; P1-2 moves fencing into 6d-a while retaining lifecycle tests in 6d-b.\n\n### 3.4 Fuel and budget propagation\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.4 completely.**\n\n**`yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`**, public, with positive exact integer `n`.\n\nOne invocation performs:\n\n1. Closed-gate check.\n2. Exactly one raw budgeted action:\n   - If control is present, `vm-hot` with fuel `n`.\n   - Otherwise, one budgeted scheduler round.\n3. If the result is blocked and an explicit bridge is installed, at most one `ffi/bridge-step`.\n4. Return that resulting machine immediately.\n\nThe implementation does **not** wrap the raw action in unchanged `ffi/maybe-run`. A handled response or malformed-request diagnostic does not restart the runner or bridge inside the same invocation. The next caller invocation resumes progress.\n\nThe bounded bridge helper may be private to the semantic runner or a bounded variant beside `ffi/maybe-run`; its contract is the four steps above. Existing unrestricted `vm/run` bridge behavior remains unchanged for live machines.\n\n**Budgeted scheduler round.**\n\nPropagate the budget through `check-wait-set`, `poll-links`, `advance-installs` and `advance-install`. Each visited child is invoked once through `run-fuel child n`; nested children use the same rule. Preserve existing gate stamping and the prohibition on running children under closed gates.\n\nAfter the sweep, resume at most one queued entry. Do not immediately run the resumed activation in that invocation. A running child retains phase `:running`; blocked children retain `:parked`; halted children complete installation as today.\n\nThe dispatch tests **control presence**, not merely `engine/active-continuation?`: the latter only tests blocked/halted flags and can classify a control-less ingress-ready machine as active. Therefore no `run-fuel` path falls through `vm-hot` into its unbudgeted scheduler fallback. A quiescent control-less machine takes the budgeted no-work round and returns.\n\n**Per-tree bound.**\n\nEach task visited during the invocation receives at most one budgeted action and at most one bridge step. Each task executes at most `n` guest instructions. For `t` distinct tasks visited during the invocation, including children created during the round, the instruction bound is `n × t`. No task is revisited with a fresh budget inside that invocation.\n\n**The honest guarantee**, copied verbatim into the documents:\n\n> Fuel counts completed guest instructions. One `run-fuel` invocation executes at most `n` instructions per visited task, at most one scheduler round per visited inactive task, and at most one bridge step per visited task. A bridge result never restarts execution inside that invocation. No wall-clock bound is claimed for an instruction, host function, stream constructor, variable resolution, bridge step or scheduler sweep. The VM has no clock and converts no time to instructions; `dao.lease` owns liveness and the composition chooses `n`.\n\n`vm/step` keeps its telemetry behavior. `run-fuel` adds no per-fuel snapshot; existing park, resume and bridge telemetry remains.\n\nRunning install children use the same carrier and receiver-admission checks as other tasks. The backends continue to refuse install children.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-1 bounds bridge execution; glm’s inactive-predicate omission is closed explicitly.\n\n### 3.5 Stack layouts at control-flow PCs; the register side\n\n**Replace v2’s displayed ordered equation and its explanatory paragraph. Retain jump/terminator derivation, stutter normalization, dead-prefix treatment and register allocation requirements.**\n\nLet:\n\n```text\nregister-sequence(p) = layout(p) ++ uses-in-order(p)\nexpected-values(p)   = [W[r] for r in register-sequence(p)]\n```\n\nAfter the separately specified dead-prefix treatment, compare the kernel region to `expected-values(p)` in emitter order. Its length equals the length of `register-sequence(p)`. Repeated occurrences of a virtual register occupy repeated positions and must decode to agreeing values.\n\nSeparately check:\n\n```text\nset(layout(p)) ∪ set(uses(p)) = L(p)   at reachable non-tail PCs\nset(uses(p))                 = L(p)   at reachable tail-call PCs\n```\n\nThere is no requirement that the length of the ordered sequence equal `|L(p)|`. Static checks validate layout availability and register sets; dynamic checks validate actual region length, order and repeated-register agreement.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-6 removes the false cardinality equation without weakening ordered reconstruction.\n\n### 3.6 Harness order, worlds, oracle, coverage\n\n**Replace the coverage and refusal-oracle paragraphs; retain v2’s lift → lower → immediate relift → law 6 → execution order and isolated worlds.**\n\n**Coverage.**\n\nThe stepping driver uses returned `run-fuel 1` boundaries. Its coverage classes are body entry, taken and untaken branch, jump, non-tail call, return, tail transition, capture, resume, park, FFI park, active-plus-waits, active-plus-ready, and running install child.\n\nBlocked-plus-ready is tested through an explicit **post-sweep/pre-resume fixture**, constructed with `check-wait-set`. It is not a mandatory returned boundary of the stepping driver. The shared-cursor fixture uses this same seam.\n\nReport driver coverage separately from constructed scheduler-product coverage. Pin per-case/per-host counts after measurement. Neither coverage inventory claims a theorem over all programs.\n\n**Refusal oracle.**\n\nThe independent test traversal covers:\n\n- Run windows and lexical bindings.\n- Every K-frame window and lexical binding.\n- Outstanding wait delivery states.\n- Ready delivery states and ready outcome values.\n- Optional ready refusal values and restored value-bearing completion fields.\n- Parked states and recursively reached collections, closures and continuations.\n\nFor register targets, exactly the expected unsupported points refuse `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` before attachment. Every other point in the admitted backend fragment transfers and continues to the reference outcome. Include a case whose only unsupported primitive is nested in a queued outcome.\n\nTerminal outcomes are compared as outcomes: receiver and source must raise the same classified failure with the same portable diagnostic data after identity normalization, rather than both being required to return a guest value.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-7; muse N4/glm O3; glm’s terminal-status acceptance omission.\n\n### 3.7 Amended document text\n\nThe v2 amendment marker and unaffected replacements remain. The following are complete replacements for the affected clauses.\n\n#### 3.7.1 `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed §2.3 ready-row entry and associated note:**\n\n> A ready row contains a delivery-state reference, completed-operation descriptor, outcome value, optional status, cursor-update row and optional raised-refusal value. Its delivery state is act or tail, never run. It is judged in context `:ready`, without an outstanding pending.\n>\n> Completed descriptors cover ordinary reads and writes, FFI response and request-append outcomes, link-request and link-response outcomes, and install outcomes. Link/install completion does not require the request transport or install entry that existed before completion. A successful link/install outcome may have no status. Try failures return their carried value; ordinary require failures restore `:link-refused` and refusal data for the terminal-resume path.\n>\n> Slot 9 is the sole cell census. A ready update pairs an existing canonical C with its independently emitted cell body. One C may name different bodies across the census and ordered update rows. Restore maps C through the census’s fresh-key map and applies the update body carried by that ready row.\n\n**Replace the proposed §3.3 frame-context sentence:**\n\n> Validate every task context first; then judge outstanding waits in `:wait`, active run states in `:running`, ready delivery states in `:ready`, parked states in `:parked`, and reified states in `:captured`. Ready descriptors receive completed-operation validation separately from pending validation. FFI call IDs are collected from outstanding waits and completed outcomes.\n\n**Replace the proposed §5.6 item 5:**\n\n> Check ordered region length and contents against `layout(p) ++ uses-in-order(p)`, with repeated-register agreement and the specified dead-prefix treatment. Check the non-tail and tail set equations separately. Ordered multiplicity is not the cardinality of `L(p)`. Retain the parked-window check.\n\n**Replace proposed §7.1 step 1:**\n\n> **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on an observe wait, held entry, unminted cursor, pending close or link request without its cursor. These are driver-owned incomplete transitions. Their completion can depend on external acknowledgement or backpressure; there is no one-cycle resolution guarantee. There is no ready-queue quiescence refusal: completed outcomes travel in order.\n\n**Replace proposed §7.1’s ready-walk clause and §7.2’s ready-restore clause:**\n\n> Each ready entry contributes its delivery state, completed descriptor, lifted outcome and optional refusal, and explicit resource-update bodies. Completed descriptors do not re-enter outstanding-pending validation.\n\n> Restore the ready queue in carried order using the task’s fresh resource keys. Restore each completion’s correlation and terminal-outcome fields. Nothing runs, polls or re-appends during lower. Fenced restoration retains the queue outside the scheduler, and the closed-gate guard prevents execution through every semantic execution entry.\n\n**Replace the proposed §7.3 retention clause:**\n\n> Stage-local execution returns the latest completed holder state explicitly on tenure failure. The ended holder retains that machine for inspection only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. Inner immutable rebinding is not a channel to an outer catch.\n\n**Replace the proposed §7.4 fuel text:** use §3.4 above, including its honest guarantee verbatim.\n\n**Replace the proposed §5.7 driver/oracle text:** use §3.6 above, including the separate post-sweep fixture and ready outcome roots.\n\n**Change:** Propagates consensus P1-1/2/3, P2-4/7/8, ordered checks, coverage hygiene and amended hold wording into normative design text.\n\n#### 3.7.2 `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed §4.2 ready-row addition:**\n\n> The running row is `:run [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`. A completed outcome is `:ready [state-cref completion-cref value optional-status updates-cref optional-refusal]`; its act/tail delivery state has no outstanding pending. Completion descriptors and cursor updates follow the continuation design’s row grammar.\n\n**Append to the proposed §4.3 portability sentence:**\n\n> Receiver refusal checks include values waiting in completed outcomes, including nested values that become live only on resume.\n\n**Change:** Synchronizes the completed grammar and P2-7 oracle roots.\n\n#### 3.7.3 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n\n**Replace §7.7.4 step 1, located by “Quiesce: the ready-queue must be empty”:**\n\n> 1. **Retain the completed outcomes.** Enter the exporting gate and move the ordered ready queue into the retained export record. The live scheduler queue is empty. Preserve every outcome value, status, completion descriptor, cursor update and raised-refusal value. No queued operation is executed, discarded or reissued by this transition.\n\n**Replace v2’s proposed step-3 guard clause:**\n\n> The semantic execution entries refuse an exporting or ended machine before instruction, scheduler or bridge execution. This guard ships with running fenced restoration in 6d-a and remains outside the instruction loop.\n\n**Replace v2’s proposed “resolve in its next cycle” sentence:**\n\n> A task holding one of these incomplete driver transitions is not export-ready. Resolution may require repeated driver cycles, external acknowledgement or relief from backpressure; no fixed cycle count is promised.\n\n**Replace the proposed retained-boundary sentence:**\n\n> A holder whose tenure ends receives the latest completed state through stage-local failure handling, retains it for inspection, and offers nothing. Recovery remains the last durable checkpoint.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P2-9 closes the omitted Quiesce step; P1-2 and P2-8 synchronize fencing and retention; amended default 8 removes the false resolution promise.\n\n#### 3.7.4 `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed ready-row, validation and completed-write clauses:**\n\n> The row grammar includes run, ready and completed-operation rows as defined in the continuation design. Ready delivery frames are validated in context `:ready`; completion descriptors are validated independently of outstanding pendings.\n>\n> The task’s slot-9 row owns the cell census. Ready update rows refer to that census and carry their own cell bodies. Canonical traversal distinguishes census ownership from update references; one C may pair with different bodies across rows.\n>\n> Completed operation IDs, when present, receive shape-only validation in the slot-18 path of `check-pendings!`. Retained-write origin, occurrence and frontier checks remain in `check-op-id!`. Completed outcomes are excluded from retained operations, numbering and `protect!`; no completed operation is reissued by lower or resume.\n>\n> The canonical round-trip law still requires no execution between lower and relift.\n\n**Change:** Consensus P1-3 and P2-4/5; corrected context inventory.\n\n#### 3.7.5–3.7.6\n\nRetain v2’s replacements. References to completed wakes mean the grammar of v2.1 §3.1; no new pending-resource requirement is implied.\n\n#### 3.7.7 `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`\n\n**Replace the proposed I-9 entry:**\n\n> **I-9 — 2026-10-16, any-PC.** The current lift rejects active control and queued completed outcomes, while the holder runs unrestricted computation. Slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e extend the carrier to run states and complete scheduler products. 6d-a includes completed stream/FFI/link/install outcomes, cursor-update encoding, bounded bridge-aware fuel and closed-gate execution guards. 6d-b adds explicit state retention on tenure failure and holder migration from fuel boundaries. 6e extends backend layouts and transfer laws. Earlier static-safepoint-only lift prescriptions are historical. No compatibility promise is added.\n\n**Change:** Makes the revision entry describe the reconciled scope and sequencing.\n\n#### 3.7.8 Assessment and known gaps\n\nRetain v2’s supersession of the assessment. Locate implementation defects by symbol and quoted text rather than obsolete offsets. The gap closes only when its corresponding acceptance gates pass; writing v2.1 does not mark implementation complete.\n\n**Change:** Citation hygiene and accurate implementation status.\n\n### 3.8 Inventory and citation policy\n\n**Replace v2’s line-offset policy and affected inventory rows.**\n\nThe source of truth is post-module-pins master. V2’s line numbers are historical locators; the “about +48” offset rule is withdrawn. For all retained inventory entries, use the named section/symbol and quoted text as the edit locator. Re-run the inventory on the implementation branch using:\n\n```text\nrg -n 'safepoint|not-at-safepoint|not-quiescent|Quiesce|quiescent' docs src test\n```\n\nHistorical logs remain append-only. Guest-event delivery safepoints remain a separate concept.\n\n| File / symbol or quoted anchor | Slice and disposition |\n|---|---|\n| `semantic.cljc` / `vm-hot`, IVM `step`, IVM `run` | 6d-a: closed-gate coverage before execution; guard outside instruction loop. |\n| `semantic.cljc` / proposed `run-fuel` | 6d-a: bounded action plus at most one bridge step; control-presence dispatch. |\n| `ffi.cljc` / `maybe-run`, “recur (run-fn vm)” | Existing unrestricted behavior retained; fuelled execution must not use this loop. |\n| `engine.cljc` / `wake-installed`, `poll-links`, `ready`, `refused-entry` | 6d-a: completed-outcome grammar covers their actual output. |\n| `rows.cljc` / `cell!`, `walk-cells`, `enter-node!`, decoded cell validation | 6d-a: one census identity; independently emitted update bodies; distinguish census from update rows. |\n| `rows.cljc` / `check-op-id!`, `check-pendings!` | 6d-a: factor shape validation; named slot-18 caller. |\n| `frame.cljc` / C3’s `:pc-body` | Keep diagnostic spelling; extend contexts with `:running` and `:ready`. |\n| `layout.cljc` / `lower-defect` | Resolve ready delivery state explicitly; inspect ready values and restored completion payloads. |\n| `driver.cljc` / `run-cycle`, `step-active` | 6d-b: stage-local failure outcomes carry the retained state. |\n| `reader.cljc` / `send-held` | Document backpressure and repeated cycles; no resolution deadline. |\n| UCF §7.7.4 / “Quiesce: the ready-queue must be empty” | 6d-b: replace with queue retention in the export record. |\n| Tuples / “Kind rules are” and “Canonical round trip” | Locate by quoted text; retain v2.1 kind rules and no-intervening-execution law. |\n\nBefore implementation sign-off, verify that no normative clause still assigns the guard to 6d-b, forbids completed link/install wakes, validates completed installs against live installs, restarts fuel through `maybe-run`, or promises one-cycle hold resolution.\n\n**Change:** muse O1/glm N3 citation hygiene; consensus P2-9; all affected code inventory entries synchronized.\n\n### 3.9 Slices, order, parallelism, effort\n\n**Replace the sequencing qualification and the 6d-a change/acceptance lists.**\n\nModule-pins commit 2 is present. Branch 6d-a from the integrated master. Remint affected fixtures once for this grammar revision.\n\n6d-a is **not described as fork-only**: it introduces the running carrier and fenced administrative lowering, and therefore ships the closed-gate guard. Holder-driven scheduling, publication and successor lifecycle remain 6d-b. After 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e may proceed independently, with 6d-b merging first if both are green.\n\n#### 6d-a: the reference lane\n\n**Change.**\n\n- Extend frame validation with run and ready contexts, correct C3 diagnostic spelling, and separate completion validation.\n- Add run, ready and completion rows; task slots 17/18; complete lift/lower/restore paths for every existing queue producer.\n- Implement update-body encoding and role-aware census traversal.\n- Factor operation-ID shape validation and invoke it from slot-18 completed validation.\n- Extend backend pre-attachment refusal traversal to ready values.\n- Add jump/terminator layouts and corrected static gates.\n- Propagate fuel through scheduler/install execution; use bounded bridge dispatch.\n- Add the closed-gate guard and cover all semantic execution entries.\n- Update the affected design text and fixtures with these behaviors.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n\n1. **Frame rules.** Run states at worked-example boundaries validate, including body start. Pin PC kind/bounds, window scope, missing/extra keys, forbidden deliver/pending and wrong-context diagnostics. C3 failures on act/return frames are `:pc-body`. Delivery frames use `saved(pc, rd)`; running frames use `L(pc)`.\n\n2. **Row grammar and completed validation.** Round-trip run frames; reject extra slots and incorrect reference kinds. Ready states admit act/tail only. Status is nil or keyword. Pin invalid completion tags, site mismatch, nonboolean link/install `try?`, inconsistent refusal slots, missing update-cell membership, duplicate update keys and malformed completed operation IDs. These lower refusals occur before attachment.\n\n3. **Task-product kinds.** Retain running-only, active-plus-waits, active-plus-ready, blocked-plus-ready and halted-plus-ready cases. Add halted-plus-outstanding-wait with empty queue: pin the existing blocked normalization and dropped result separately. Retain invalid run counts, illegal result/parked-id and empty-blocked cases.\n\n4. **Completed link/install outcomes.** Exercise success, try failure and ordinary raised failure for link/install producers. Capture completed install outcomes after removal of the install entry. Include active control beside a remaining completion. Immediate relift is equal; continuation delivers or raises once, without reconstructing retired pending resources.\n\n5. **Shared cursor.** Two waiters share one cursor. Snapshot after the sweep before any resume. Preserve one canonical cell identity, the slot-9 current body, both values and update rows in order. Assert immediate relift equality and source/receiver continuation equality. Add an encoding case where one C pairs with different bodies across census/update rows.\n\n6. **Completed write and terminal outcomes.** Fork and exclusive completed-write histories round-trip without enrollment or reappend. A malformed present ID refuses at the named slot-18 check. Nil IDs remain admissible where the ungated source produced none. Add a terminal-status ready case whose receiver raises identically, plus the FFI sent-request transition that creates a response wait without a second request append.\n\n7. **Every returned boundary, slow tier.** Run the existing corpus through `run-fuel 1`; lift, lower into isolated worlds, immediately relift, validate frames and continue to the reference outcome. Pin measured counts and the driver coverage classes of §3.6. Constructed post-sweep cases are reported separately.\n\n8. **Quick sample and products.** Retain v2’s structural sample and every-boundary small rows. Run the scheduler products and shared-cursor seam through the harness. Retain cursor-ref, reified-continuation, nil-live-register, `ghost` and hold-refusal cases.\n\n9. **Layout gates.** At every reachable corpus PC, check register injectivity, jump/terminator layout availability and separate non-tail/tail set equations. On executed paths compare ordered region length and values to the ordered register sequence, including repeated-register agreement. Do not compare that sequence length to `|L(pc)|`.\n\n10. **Budgeted children.** Preempt long and nested initializers; lift/lower running children under receiver admission; continue to linked. Count actual instruction transitions and assert at most `n` per visited task and `n × t` overall. No child receives a second budget inside one invocation.\n\n11. **Bounded bridge execution.** A program repeatedly making bridge-served FFI calls returns from every `run-fuel` invocation. Each invocation executes at most one raw action and one bridge step. Repeated invocations reach the reference result. Diagnostic-only bridge input also returns after one step. Pin control-less ingress-ready dispatch to the budgeted round. For a pure active computation lasting at least `n` instructions, compare with `n` single-instruction steps; an earlier park/halt ends the comparison there.\n\n12. **Fencing.** Lower a running snapshot with `{:fenced true}` and exercise direct `vm-hot`, `vm/step`, `vm/run` and `run-fuel`. Each refuses the closed gate before guest, scheduler or bridge work. Assert unchanged control/window/environment/K, unchanged ready recovery data and unchanged relift bytes. Cover `:ended` as well as `:exporting`.\n\n13. **Ready-value portability.** The only unsupported primitive is nested in the ready outcome; register-target lower refuses before attachment. Retain the corresponding portable positive case. Test ready delivery state, outcome and restored descriptor roots independently.\n\n14. **Landing checks.** Kondo clean; the quick tier passes on JVM, Node and Dart with every touched namespace present in logs; the slow tier runs once at landing on all three hosts. Remint fixtures from the integrated grammar. Review stops on satisfied gates, not a fixed round count.\n\n**Change:** All three P1s and all reconciled P2/P3 acceptance amendments are now explicit 6d-a gates.\n\n#### 6d-b: holder, custody, expiry, recovery\n\n**Replace v2’s numbered change list.**\n\n1. Add positive-integer-or-nil holder `:fuel`; use `run-fuel` when configured.\n2. Implement §3.3’s stage-local state-bearing failure channel and exact retained-machine diagnostics.\n3. Update export classification, queue retention, recovery metadata, abort/reinstatement and fenced rehydration for completed outcomes.\n4. Extend ledger/grant offerable kinds with `:running`.\n5. Retain the execution-entry guard from 6d-a; add holder integration tests rather than introducing the guard here.\n6. Keep v2’s custody race, grant, expiry, competing-candidate and Stage E tests; add exact retained-machine equality at every cut.\n7. For each hold class, test whether normal conditions resolve it in one cycle. Record any failure of that property in known gaps, including a backpressure fixture that deliberately persists across cycles. Do not make one-cycle resolution a runtime promise.\n8. Apply the 6d-b document amendments, including replacement of UCF’s Quiesce step.\n\n**Change:** P1-2 sequencing, P2-8 state transport, P2-9 documentation and amended default 8.\n\n#### 6e: the cross-engine half\n\nRetain v2’s scope and acceptance, replacing references to its oracle and cardinality equation with §§3.5–3.6 above. Ready handling uses the completed-outcome grammar, not pending-wait reconstruction. The backend fragment and existing obstruction list remain unchanged.\n\n**Change:** Propagates ready-value and ordered-layout corrections into cross-engine gates.\n\n#### Effort and routing\n\nThe v2 estimates remain planning estimates: 4.5 days for 6d-a, 4 for 6d-b, 5 for 6e, plus the stated documentation allocation. The added gates are not optional to preserve those estimates.\n\nThis v2.1 draft is by codex; glm confirms it. Fable diff-confirms Tuesday 2026-10-20 before any merge, as commissioned. Implementation assignment remains the orchestrator’s responsibility. No merge or implementation completion is asserted by this document.\n\n**Change:** Aligns routing with the owner’s current commission and preserves gate-based review.\n\n### 3.10 Decisions for the owner — ratified defaults\n\n**Replaces v2 §3.10 completely.**\n\n1. **Task slots 17 (`run`) and 18 (`ready`).** Keep separate typed slots and remint affected roots after module-pins commit 2. This removes mixed-slot positional misreads.\n\n2. **Holder `:fuel`, default nil.** Nil retains current unrestricted behavior; compositions opt into instruction-budget preemption explicitly.\n\n3. **No automatic export at the bound in 6d-b.** `hand-off` remains explicit and `:yin.k/at-boundary` supplies the signal. A later tenure-aware automatic policy belongs in composition.\n\n4. **No adoption of a retained boundary.** Ended holders retain inspection data only. Recovery uses the last durable checkpoint; progress since it is lost. A later same-holder regrant/adoption rule would be new ledger authority.\n\n5. **Keep Q6 edge-reachability liveness.** Pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal. Constant propagation remains a separate slice.\n\n6. **Completed `:put` wakes are completed outcomes, excluded from retained writes and `protect!`, and never re-appended.** Separate operation-ID shape validation from retained-write custody validation. Factor `check-op-id-shape!` from `check-op-id!`; call it from `check-pendings!` over slot 18 for every present completed operation ID. The retained-write path additionally keeps its origin, occurrence and sequence-frontier checks. Completed fork history requires no root custody context. Pin malformed-ID refusal and valid fork/exclusive completed histories.\n\n7. **Relabel stack `:push` to its successor.** No guest semantics run; the value is already on the stack.\n\n8. **Holds remain refusals.** Observe entries, held observations, unminted cursors, pending closes and link requests without installed cursors remain incomplete driver transitions. Backpressure or acknowledgement delay may preserve them across arbitrarily many cycles. The documents promise no one-cycle resolution. 6d-b still tests each hold’s one-cycle behavior under stated fixture conditions and records any nonresolving case in known gaps; that reporting duty does not convert external progress into a guarantee.\n\n**Change:** Writes the owner-ratified wording directly into defaults 6 and 8; defaults 1–5 and 7 retain their policies.\n\n### 3.11 Risks\n\n**Replace v2 risk 2 and append the following qualifications.**\n\n2. **Queue producer completeness.** Every existing producer must have a completed descriptor and acceptance case in 6d-a. Discovering another existing producer is a defect to fix before that slice lands, not permission to ship `:ready-reason` refusal for supported execution.\n\n8. **Cursor traversal coupling.** Adding update rows requires auditing every census-special-case traversal. Passing ordinary next/put cases alone does not establish canonical numbering for shared identities.\n\n9. **External progress.** Fuel bounds guest instruction work, not host duration, acknowledgement latency or hold resolution. Expiry recovery remains checkpoint-based.\n\n10. **Stage failures.** Retention preserves the last completed stage. It does not claim transactional rollback or recovery of an operation’s unreturned intermediate locals.\n\n**Change:** Removes the link-wake deferral and records the reconciled limits without weakening acceptance.\n\n## 4. Verification honesty\n\n**Replaces v2 §4 completely for this amendment edition.**\n\nThe consensus, muse findings, glm findings and v2 were read completely in the requested order. New implementation citations below were checked against this directory’s tree. Proposed helpers and row kinds are specifications, not claims that those definitions already exist.\n\n| Verified site | Relevant fact |\n|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:278` and `:334` | `bridge-step` performs one bridge operation; `maybe-run` can repeat runner/bridge work. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:137`, `:271`, `:331` | Hot-loop fallback, scheduler wrapper and public protocol execution paths. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:258` | `active-continuation?` checks flags, not control presence. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1148` | Link completion removes envelope/request/response/cursor fields. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1156` | Try failures return data; ordinary failures retain status and refusal. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1327`, `:1627` | Install and link outcomes enter the ready queue. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:49` | Existing row descriptors support act/tail references and discriminated pending rows. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1243` | Operation-ID shape and custody checks currently share one function. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1388` | `cell!` is first-wins per source identity. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:397`, `:468`, `:819` | Existing traversal skips are keyed by `:cursor-cells` kind. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1131` | Task validation currently gathers FFI IDs from waits. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:2242` | Restore reconstructs pending entries and FFI response transitions. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc:162` | Existing context, site/reason and try validation paths. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:2117`, `:2252` | Cycle locals and outer failure handling are separate scopes. |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/reader.cljc:484` | Full append preserves the held observation for later cycles. |\n| `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1904` | The omitted Quiesce requirement exists. |\n\nNo new code citation is knowingly unverified. Inherited v2 offsets are explicitly historical; symbol/quoted-text locators govern their application. Corpus counts, revised point counts, runtime bounds and cross-host behavior remain acceptance work, not executed evidence.\n\n## 5. Readiness\n\n6d-a is implementation-ready under the ratified defaults and the complete replacement text above. Module-pins commit 2 is already present. The bounded bridge path, execution-entry fencing and complete link/install outcome grammar are mandatory 6d-a work.\n\n6d-b and 6e depend on the resulting grammar. Holder-level closure is claimed only after 6d-b’s lifecycle gates pass. Fable’s required diff confirmation remains a pre-merge gate.\n\n**Residual risks**\n\n- **Halted plus outstanding waits:** the consensus explicitly retains today’s blocked normalization and dropped halt value. The source’s `vm/value` can nevertheless expose that value before further scheduling. V2’s blanket law-1 wording must not be treated as proof of equality for that immediate inspection. V2.1 pins the existing normalization separately; any expansion of the observation contract requires an explicit ruling.\n- **V2’s act/run acceptance wording:** its phrase “an act … and a run … with rd present both validate” is ambiguous and, if applied to the act window, conflicts with `saved(pc, rd)`. Acceptance item 1 now names the two window sets explicitly; reviewers should confirm that this is the intended reading.\n- **Static versus dynamic evidence:** actual stack-region contents cannot be established by a static layout-only test. V2.1 separates static set/layout checks from executed ordered-region checks. Neither is presented as a universal proof.\n\nPLAN-V2.1-READY"}}
